{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-bd47cae919ecee24", "type": "tool_result", "content": "100\tcaption [12–14]. Our structured caption normalizes heterogeneous annotations into one interface for speech content and\n101\tattributes, music, sound events, and ambience. Unlike explicit\n102\ttimeline planning, it supplies a common scene description;\n103\tCPE-MoE, the core module of SonicWeave, uses this global\n104\tinformation as a routing prior and combines it with evolving\n105\tlocal acoustic evidence.\n106\t\n107\t• We identify conditional computation at two coupled granularities—across audio domains and within heterogeneous\n108\tscenes—and formulate routing requirements that jointly\n109\taccount for global scene intent, local acoustic state, and\n110\tshort-range temporal continuity.\n111\t• We introduce CPE-MoE, which routes contiguous acoustic chunks using a diffusion-phase-aware text prior, local\n112\tacoustic evidence, and a learned conflict gate, while keeping text and time tokens on a stable shared path.\n113\t\n114\t2.3 Mixture-of-Experts for Diffusion and Audio\n115\tGeneration\n116\t\n117\t• Through matched Dense and token-routed MoE controls,\n118\tpublic-task benchmarks, a 100-prompt complex-scene\n119\tsuite, human listening tests, and routing analyses, we\n120\tshow that the proposed routing improves compositional\n121\tfidelity without sacrificing perceptual quality.\n122\t\n123\t2\n124\t\n125\tRelated Work\n126\t\n127\t2.1\n128\t\n129\tGeneral and Unified Audio Generation\n130\t\n131\tStructured Text Conditioning\n132\t\n133\tMoE enables conditional capacity through sparse expert activation and has been adapted to diffusion transformers [15–18].\n134\tIn unified audio generation, UniMoE-Audio uses specialistinitialized experts and dynamic allocation to mitigate speech–\n135\tmusic task conflict [9]. These approaches establish the value\n136\tof specialization, but operate at task/modality granularity or\n137\troute individual tokens. SonicWeave instead uses CPE-MoE to\n138\troute contiguous acoustic chunks with a conflict gate between a\n139\tglobal text-and-phase prior and local evidence, targeting stable,\n140\tfine-grained specialization in heterogeneous mixed scenes.\n141\t\n142\tText-conditioned audio generation has progressed from autoregressive audio language models to diffusion and flow-matching\n143\t2\n144\t\n145\t\fFigure 2: SonicWeave framework. Left: a stereo VAE encodes audio into continuous latents, which are jointly modeled with a structured-caption\n146\tstream in a flow-matching DiT. Right: the CPE-MoE module groups audio tokens into chunks and fuses a global text-and-phase prior with local\n147\tacoustic evidence for sparse expert routing.\n148\t\n149\t3\n150\t\n151\tMethod\n152\t\n153\t3.1\n154\t\n155\tOverview\n156\t\n157\t3.3\n158\t\n159\tSequence Construction.\n160\tEach transformer layer operates on the concatenation of three\n161\tmodality groups:\n162\t\u0002\n163\t\u0003\n164\th = ttime ∥ T1:L ∥ A1:T ∈ R(1+L+T )×d .\n165\t(1)\n166\t|{z} |{z} |{z}\n167\t\n168\tOur dual-stream conditional flow-matching generator, SonicWeave, jointly models audio and text. In the audio stream, a\n169\tstereo waveform is encoded by a pretrained stereo VAE into\n170\tcontinuous audio latents; the model denoises a noised latent\n171\ttogether with an optional masked reference for continuation\n172\tand inpainting. In the text stream, a frozen language encoder\n173\tembeds a structured audio caption. A joint audio–text diffusion\n174\ttransformer (DiT) then predicts the flow velocity, with the CPEMoE module replacing the FFN in its final layers. Figure 2\n175\tsummarizes the SonicWeave architecture and its CPE-MoE\n176\tblock.\n177\t\n178\t3.2\n179\t\n180\tJoint Audio–Text DiT Backbone\n181\t\n182\t1\n183\t\n184\ttext\n185\t\n186\taudio\n187\t\n188\tThe time token ttime is obtained by summing sinusoidal embeddings for diffusion time t and segment start s, then projecting\n189\tthe result to d. Text tokens are T = Wcap E, where E denotes\n190\tthe frozen language-encoder outputs. Audio tokens concatenate the noised stereo-VAE latent xt with the masked reference\n191\tc, followed by linear projection and convolutional positional\n192\tembedding.\n193\t\n194\tStructured Text Conditioning\n195\t\n196\tModality-Decoupled RoPE.\n197\tInspired by the modality-specific coordinate design of M-RoPE\n198\t[19], we assign text and audio independent one-dimensional\n199\tposition axes. The time token uses position 0, while text and\n200\taudio positions are reset separately:\n201\t\n202\tWe convert each natural-language annotation into a\n203\tcompact, audio-only structured caption. An automatic\n204\tannotation pipeline first removes non-acoustic details and\n205\textracts a fixed set of fields: type, language, speech text\n206\tor lyrics, speaker counts, speaker and vocal attributes,\n207\tmusic, sound effect, ambience, and recording texture.\n208\tThe fields are serialized with explicit tags, e.g., Summary\n209\tsentence. <type>speech</type> <lang>en</lang>\n210\t<speech>...</speech> <music>...</music>.\n211\tThis factorized format separates otherwise entangled scene\n212\tattributes, preserves linguistic content verbatim, and supplies a\n213\tconsistent interface across speech, music, sound effects, and\n214\tmixed audio. The current schema explicitly supports up to\n215\ttwo speakers for dialogue-oriented scenes. A frozen language\n216\tencoder maps the resulting caption into text tokens for the\n217\tDiT; we use one shared encoder rather than domain-specific\n218\tconditioning modules.\n219\t\n220\tpos(ttime ) = 0, pos(Ti ) = i, pos(A j ) = j.\n221\t\n222\t(2)\n223\t\n224\tWith this modality-specific coordinate system, positional encoding preserves the intrinsic order of both text and audio while\n225\tkeeping their coordinates independent of sequence length in the\n226\tother modality. It also provides a consistent relative-position\n227\tstructure for joint attention to model cross-modal correspondences.\n228\tText-Conditioned AdaLN.\n229\tJoint self-attention provides token-level text–audio interaction,\n230\twhile adaptive normalization offers a complementary path for\n231\t3\n232\t\n233\t\finjecting global conditions into diffusion transformers [20]. We\n234\tadditionally use phase-aware attention pooling over the structured caption to provide global AdaLN conditioning; details\n235\tare given in the appendix.\n236\t\n237\tchunk. The selected experts nevertheless transform the original\n238\tframe states, not the pooled state e j . Thus, pooling controls\n239\tonly expert selection: frame-level modeling is retained while\n240\tneighboring frames share a locally consistent computation path.\n241\tImportantly, the chunk is the routing unit rather than the representation unit: expert selection is shared within a chunk, but\n242\teach expert still transforms the original frame-level states.\n243\t\n244\tCross-Modal Alignment Loss.\n245\tThe shared DiT processes audio and text jointly, but flow matching alone does not explicitly require their final hidden states to\n246\tbe aligned. We therefore add a symmetric in-batch contrastive\n247\tobjective, following the paired-representation alignment principle used in CLIP [21]. Let ab and rb be the ℓ2 -normalized,\n248\tmask-pooled audio and text states of sample b, respectively,\n249\tand define Sbb′ = exp(λ ) a⊤\n250\tb rb′ , where λ is learned. We minimize\n251\th\n252\ti\n253\t(3)\n254\tLCL = 12 CE(S, y) + CE(S⊤ , y) , yb = b.\n255\t\n256\tPrior and Evidence Signals.\n257\tThe router combines two complementary signals with different\n258\tinformation scopes for each chunk.\n259\tPrior. A global, chunk-independent signal that summarizes\n260\twhat to generate and at which diffusion phase:\n261\tp = [ttime ∥ T̄] ∈ R2d , ℓprior = Wprior (p) ∈ RNe ,\n262\t\n263\t(6)\n264\t\n265\twhere T̄ is the mask-pooled text hidden state at the current layer.\n266\tBecause p carries both the text summary and the diffusion-step\n267\tembedding, ℓprior is a task- and step-aware expert preference\n268\tthat summarizes the global generation intent.\n269\tEvidence. A local signal is computed from the current contextconditioned audio representation e j :\n270\t\n271\tThis aligns paired audio–caption representations and supports\n272\tthe text-derived prior and audio-derived evidence used by CPEMoE.\n273\t\n274\t3.4 CPE-MoE: Conflict-Gated Prior–Evidence\n275\tChunk Routing\n276\t\n277\tℓevid\n278\t= Wevid (e j ) ∈ RNe .\n279\tj\n280\t\n281\tThe CPE-MoE module replaces the dense FFN in the final\n282\ttransformer layers with shared and sparsely routed experts, providing conditional capacity for heterogeneous audio. As shown\n283\tin Figure 2, it learns routing from two complementary sources:\n284\tstructured text and diffusion phase specify global generation\n285\tintent, while evolving audio states reveal local acoustic content. Routing contiguous chunks preserves short-range audio\n286\tcontinuity, allowing expert specialization across broad tasks\n287\tand locally mixed content under one objective.\n288\t\n289\t(7)\n290\t\n291\tBecause e j is pooled after joint self-attention, it reflects the\n292\tacoustic state currently emerging in chunk j. The evidence\n293\tlogits can thus capture local departures from the scene-level\n294\tdescription, such as a transient event within speech or music.\n295\tBoth projections are 2-layer MLPs with SiLU activation\n296\tand zero-initialized output biases, avoiding an explicit expert\n297\tpreference at initialization.\n298\tConflict Gate and Fusion.\n299\t\n300\tModality-Decoupled Dispatch.\n301\t\n302\tThe prior gives a global expert preference, but local audio\n303\tbecomes informative at different rates across diffusion phases\n304\tand acoustic regions. We therefore let each chunk decide how\n305\tfar its routing should move from the prior toward the local\n306\tevidence. We first project the semantic–phase summary into\n307\tthe d-dimensional hidden space of the joint DiT:\n308\t\n309\tGiven the layer input h ∈ RB×(1+L+T )×d , we split it into a\n310\tcontext group (time and text tokens) and an audio group:\n311\thctx = h[:, :1+L] , haud = h[:, −T :] .\n312\t\n313\t(4)\n314\t\n315\tThe context group is processed only by the shared expert octx =\n316\tMLPshared (hctx ). This isolates the text and time representations\n317\tfrom the routing gate.\n318\t\n319\tp̃ = Walign p ∈ Rd .\n320\t\n321\tThe gate observes both signals and their explicit pairwise\n322\tinteractions:\n323\t\n324\tChunk-Level Routing.\n325\tWe partition the T audio states into Nc = ⌈T /C⌉ nonc\n326\toverlapping chunks {C j }Nj=1\n327\tof at most C consecutive frames.\n328\tLet mi ∈ {0, 1} indicate whether frame i is valid. The pooled\n329\tstate of chunk j is\n330\t∑i∈C j mi haud,i\n331\t\u0010\n332\t\u0011.\n333\tej =\n334\tmax 1, ∑i∈C j mi\n335\t\n336\t(8)\n337\t\n338\tφ j = LN([ p̃ ∥ e j ∥ p̃⊙e j ∥ |p̃ − e j | ]) ,\n339\t\n340\t(9)\n341\t\n342\t\u0010\n343\t\u0011\n344\tg j = σ MLPg (φ j ) .\n345\t\n346\t(10)\n347\t\n348\tFollowing pairwise matching features [31], the product exposes coordinate-wise agreement and the absolute difference\n349\texposes disagreement, while the original vectors retain sourcespecific information. The MLP therefore learns how agreement\n350\tor conflict should affect routing rather than imposing a fixed\n351\tsimilarity rule; this is useful because discrepancy may reflect\n352\teither a meaningful local event or noisy evidence depending on\n353\t\n354\t(5)\n355\t\n356\tPadding-only chunks are excluded from routing and auxiliary\n357\tstatistics. The prior–evidence router makes one top-K assignment per valid chunk and broadcasts it to all frames in that\n358\t4\n359\t\n360\t\fTable 1: Comparison with representative specialized and general-purpose audio generators. ✓ indicates explicitly reported support; △ denotes\n361\tpartial support, limited public demonstrations or support under restricted settings; and – denotes no explicit evidence of support found in the\n362\tpublic documentation we reviewed. \"Background\" includes music, sound effects, environmental ambience, or their combinations.\n363\t\n364\tCategory\n365\t\n366\tGeneral tasks\n367\t\n368\tModel\n369\t\n370\tComplex-scene generation\n371\t\n372\tTTS\n373\t\n374\tTTA\n375\t\n376\tTTM\n377\t\n378\tSpeech+Bg.\n379\t\n380\tSing+Bg.\n381\t\n382\tDialog\n383\t\n384\tDialog+Bg.\n385\t\n386\tSpecialized\n387\t\n388\tCosyVoice 2 [22]\n389\tF5-TTS [3]\n390\tStep-Audio [23]\n391\tTangoFlux [4]\n392\tMusicGen-Large [24]\n393\tSegTune [25]\n394\t\n395\t✓\n396\t✓\n397\t✓\n398\t–\n399\t–\n400\t–\n401\t\n402\t–\n403\t–\n404\t–\n405\t✓\n406\t–\n407\t–\n408\t\n409\t–\n410\t–\n411\t–\n412\t–\n413\t✓\n414\t✓\n415\t\n416\t–\n417\t–\n418\t–\n419\t–\n420\t–\n421\t–\n422\t\n423\t–\n424\t–\n425\t–\n426\t–\n427\t–\n428\t△\n429\t\n430\t–\n431\t–\n432\t△\n433\t–\n434\t–\n435\t–\n436\t\n437\t–\n438\t–\n439\t–\n440\t–\n441\t–\n442\t–\n443\t\n444\tGeneral / multi-task\n445\t\n446\tQwen2.5-Omni [26]\n447\tAudioLDM 2 [5]\n448\tStable Audio Open [27]\n449\tUniAudio [6]\n450\tAudioX [28]\n451\tHiggs Audio V2 [29]\n452\tUniMoE-Audio [9]\n453\tUniFlow-Audio [30]\n454\tDasheng AudioGen [8]\n455\t\n456\t✓\n457\t△\n458\t–\n459\t✓\n460\t–\n461\t✓\n462\t✓\n463\t✓\n464\t✓\n465\t\n466\t△\n467\t✓\n468\t✓\n469\t✓\n470\t✓\n471\t✓\n472\t–\n473\t✓\n474\t✓\n475\t\n476\t△\n477\t△\n478\t✓\n479\t✓\n480\t✓\n481\t△\n482\t✓\n483\t✓\n484\t✓\n485\t\n486\t–\n487\t△\n488\t–\n489\t△\n490\t–\n491\t△\n492\t–\n493\t△\n494\t✓\n495\t\n496\t–\n497\t△\n498\t△\n499\t–\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "99f9f2f5-463f-4d73-8c1b-6ee352cfb6b2", "uuid": "a2864a91-54b8-43ab-9ef5-fa176f50ab62", "timestamp": "2026-08-11T14:44:27.874Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/sonicweave.txt", "content": "caption [12–14]. Our structured caption normalizes heterogeneous annotations into one interface for speech content and\nattributes, music, sound events, and ambience. Unlike explicit\ntimeline planning, it supplies a common scene description;\nCPE-MoE, the core module of SonicWeave, uses this global\ninformation as a routing prior and combines it with evolving\nlocal acoustic evidence.\n\n• We identify conditional computation at two coupled granularities—across audio domains and within heterogeneous\nscenes—and formulate routing requirements that jointly\naccount for global scene intent, local acoustic state, and\nshort-range temporal continuity.\n• We introduce CPE-MoE, which routes contiguous acoustic chunks using a diffusion-phase-aware text prior, local\nacoustic evidence, and a learned conflict gate, while keeping text and time tokens on a stable shared path.\n\n2.3 Mixture-of-Experts for Diffusion and Audio\nGeneration\n\n• Through matched Dense and token-routed MoE controls,\npublic-task benchmarks, a 100-prompt complex-scene\nsuite, human listening tests, and routing analyses, we\nshow that the proposed routing improves compositional\nfidelity without sacrificing perceptual quality.\n\n2\n\nRelated Work\n\n2.1\n\nGeneral and Unified Audio Generation\n\nStructured Text Conditioning\n\nMoE enables conditional capacity through sparse expert activation and has been adapted to diffusion transformers [15–18].\nIn unified audio generation, UniMoE-Audio uses specialistinitialized experts and dynamic allocation to mitigate speech–\nmusic task conflict [9]. These approaches establish the value\nof specialization, but operate at task/modality granularity or\nroute individual tokens. SonicWeave instead uses CPE-MoE to\nroute contiguous acoustic chunks with a conflict gate between a\nglobal text-and-phase prior and local evidence, targeting stable,\nfine-grained specialization in heterogeneous mixed scenes.\n\nText-conditioned audio generation has progressed from autoregressive audio language models to diffusion and flow-matching\n2\n\n\fFigure 2: SonicWeave framework. Left: a stereo VAE encodes audio into continuous latents, which are jointly modeled with a structured-caption\nstream in a flow-matching DiT. Right: the CPE-MoE module groups audio tokens into chunks and fuses a global text-and-phase prior with local\nacoustic evidence for sparse expert routing.\n\n3\n\nMethod\n\n3.1\n\nOverview\n\n3.3\n\nSequence Construction.\nEach transformer layer operates on the concatenation of three\nmodality groups:\n\u0002\n\u0003\nh = ttime ∥ T1:L ∥ A1:T ∈ R(1+L+T )×d .\n(1)\n|{z} |{z} |{z}\n\nOur dual-stream conditional flow-matching generator, SonicWeave, jointly models audio and text. In the audio stream, a\nstereo waveform is encoded by a pretrained stereo VAE into\ncontinuous audio latents; the model denoises a noised latent\ntogether with an optional masked reference for continuation\nand inpainting. In the text stream, a frozen language encoder\nembeds a structured audio caption. A joint audio–text diffusion\ntransformer (DiT) then predicts the flow velocity, with the CPEMoE module replacing the FFN in its final layers. Figure 2\nsummarizes the SonicWeave architecture and its CPE-MoE\nblock.\n\n3.2\n\nJoint Audio–Text DiT Backbone\n\n1\n\ntext\n\naudio\n\nThe time token ttime is obtained by summing sinusoidal embeddings for diffusion time t and segment start s, then projecting\nthe result to d. Text tokens are T = Wcap E, where E denotes\nthe frozen language-encoder outputs. Audio tokens concatenate the noised stereo-VAE latent xt with the masked reference\nc, followed by linear projection and convolutional positional\nembedding.\n\nStructured Text Conditioning\n\nModality-Decoupled RoPE.\nInspired by the modality-specific coordinate design of M-RoPE\n[19], we assign text and audio independent one-dimensional\nposition axes. The time token uses position 0, while text and\naudio positions are reset separately:\n\nWe convert each natural-language annotation into a\ncompact, audio-only structured caption. An automatic\nannotation pipeline first removes non-acoustic details and\nextracts a fixed set of fields: type, language, speech text\nor lyrics, speaker counts, speaker and vocal attributes,\nmusic, sound effect, ambience, and recording texture.\nThe fields are serialized with explicit tags, e.g., Summary\nsentence. <type>speech</type> <lang>en</lang>\n<speech>...</speech> <music>...</music>.\nThis factorized format separates otherwise entangled scene\nattributes, preserves linguistic content verbatim, and supplies a\nconsistent interface across speech, music, sound effects, and\nmixed audio. The current schema explicitly supports up to\ntwo speakers for dialogue-oriented scenes. A frozen language\nencoder maps the resulting caption into text tokens for the\nDiT; we use one shared encoder rather than domain-specific\nconditioning modules.\n\npos(ttime ) = 0, pos(Ti ) = i, pos(A j ) = j.\n\n(2)\n\nWith this modality-specific coordinate system, positional encoding preserves the intrinsic order of both text and audio while\nkeeping their coordinates independent of sequence length in the\nother modality. It also provides a consistent relative-position\nstructure for joint attention to model cross-modal correspondences.\nText-Conditioned AdaLN.\nJoint self-attention provides token-level text–audio interaction,\nwhile adaptive normalization offers a complementary path for\n3\n\n\finjecting global conditions into diffusion transformers [20]. We\nadditionally use phase-aware attention pooling over the structured caption to provide global AdaLN conditioning; details\nare given in the appendix.\n\nchunk. The selected experts nevertheless transform the original\nframe states, not the pooled state e j . Thus, pooling controls\nonly expert selection: frame-level modeling is retained while\nneighboring frames share a locally consistent computation path.\nImportantly, the chunk is the routing unit rather than the representation unit: expert selection is shared within a chunk, but\neach expert still transforms the original frame-level states.\n\nCross-Modal Alignment Loss.\nThe shared DiT processes audio and text jointly, but flow matching alone does not explicitly require their final hidden states to\nbe aligned. We therefore add a symmetric in-batch contrastive\nobjective, following the paired-representation alignment principle used in CLIP [21]. Let ab and rb be the ℓ2 -normalized,\nmask-pooled audio and text states of sample b, respectively,\nand define Sbb′ = exp(λ ) a⊤\nb rb′ , where λ is learned. We minimize\nh\ni\n(3)\nLCL = 12 CE(S, y) + CE(S⊤ , y) , yb = b.\n\nPrior and Evidence Signals.\nThe router combines two complementary signals with different\ninformation scopes for each chunk.\nPrior. A global, chunk-independent signal that summarizes\nwhat to generate and at which diffusion phase:\np = [ttime ∥ T̄] ∈ R2d , ℓprior = Wprior (p) ∈ RNe ,\n\n(6)\n\nwhere T̄ is the mask-pooled text hidden state at the current layer.\nBecause p carries both the text summary and the diffusion-step\nembedding, ℓprior is a task- and step-aware expert preference\nthat summarizes the global generation intent.\nEvidence. A local signal is computed from the current contextconditioned audio representation e j :\n\nThis aligns paired audio–caption representations and supports\nthe text-derived prior and audio-derived evidence used by CPEMoE.\n\n3.4 CPE-MoE: Conflict-Gated Prior–Evidence\nChunk Routing\n\nℓevid\n= Wevid (e j ) ∈ RNe .\nj\n\nThe CPE-MoE module replaces the dense FFN in the final\ntransformer layers with shared and sparsely routed experts, providing conditional capacity for heterogeneous audio. As shown\nin Figure 2, it learns routing from two complementary sources:\nstructured text and diffusion phase specify global generation\nintent, while evolving audio states reveal local acoustic content. Routing contiguous chunks preserves short-range audio\ncontinuity, allowing expert specialization across broad tasks\nand locally mixed content under one objective.\n\n(7)\n\nBecause e j is pooled after joint self-attention, it reflects the\nacoustic state currently emerging in chunk j. The evidence\nlogits can thus capture local departures from the scene-level\ndescription, such as a transient event within speech or music.\nBoth projections are 2-layer MLPs with SiLU activation\nand zero-initialized output biases, avoiding an explicit expert\npreference at initialization.\nConflict Gate and Fusion.\n\nModality-Decoupled Dispatch.\n\nThe prior gives a global expert preference, but local audio\nbecomes informative at different rates across diffusion phases\nand acoustic regions. We therefore let each chunk decide how\nfar its routing should move from the prior toward the local\nevidence. We first project the semantic–phase summary into\nthe d-dimensional hidden space of the joint DiT:\n\nGiven the layer input h ∈ RB×(1+L+T )×d , we split it into a\ncontext group (time and text tokens) and an audio group:\nhctx = h[:, :1+L] , haud = h[:, −T :] .\n\n(4)\n\nThe context group is processed only by the shared expert octx =\nMLPshared (hctx ). This isolates the text and time representations\nfrom the routing gate.\n\np̃ = Walign p ∈ Rd .\n\nThe gate observes both signals and their explicit pairwise\ninteractions:\n\nChunk-Level Routing.\nWe partition the T audio states into Nc = ⌈T /C⌉ nonc\noverlapping chunks {C j }Nj=1\nof at most C consecutive frames.\nLet mi ∈ {0, 1} indicate whether frame i is valid. The pooled\nstate of chunk j is\n∑i∈C j mi haud,i\n\u0010\n\u0011.\nej =\nmax 1, ∑i∈C j mi\n\n(8)\n\nφ j = LN([ p̃ ∥ e j ∥ p̃⊙e j ∥ |p̃ − e j | ]) ,\n\n(9)\n\n\u0010\n\u0011\ng j = σ MLPg (φ j ) .\n\n(10)\n\nFollowing pairwise matching features [31], the product exposes coordinate-wise agreement and the absolute difference\nexposes disagreement, while the original vectors retain sourcespecific information. The MLP therefore learns how agreement\nor conflict should affect routing rather than imposing a fixed\nsimilarity rule; this is useful because discrepancy may reflect\neither a meaningful local event or noisy evidence depending on\n\n(5)\n\nPadding-only chunks are excluded from routing and auxiliary\nstatistics. The prior–evidence router makes one top-K assignment per valid chunk and broadcasts it to all frames in that\n4\n\n\fTable 1: Comparison with representative specialized and general-purpose audio generators. ✓ indicates explicitly reported support; △ denotes\npartial support, limited public demonstrations or support under restricted settings; and – denotes no explicit evidence of support found in the\npublic documentation we reviewed. \"Background\" includes music, sound effects, environmental ambience, or their combinations.\n\nCategory\n\nGeneral tasks\n\nModel\n\nComplex-scene generation\n\nTTS\n\nTTA\n\nTTM\n\nSpeech+Bg.\n\nSing+Bg.\n\nDialog\n\nDialog+Bg.\n\nSpecialized\n\nCosyVoice 2 [22]\nF5-TTS [3]\nStep-Audio [23]\nTangoFlux [4]\nMusicGen-Large [24]\nSegTune [25]\n\n✓\n✓\n✓\n–\n–\n–\n\n–\n–\n–\n✓\n–\n–\n\n–\n–\n–\n–\n✓\n✓\n\n–\n–\n–\n–\n–\n–\n\n–\n–\n–\n–\n–\n△\n\n–\n–\n△\n–\n–\n–\n\n–\n–\n–\n–\n–\n–\n\nGeneral / multi-task\n\nQwen2.5-Omni [26]\nAudioLDM 2 [5]\nStable Audio Open [27]\nUniAudio [6]\nAudioX [28]\nHiggs Audio V2 [29]\nUniMoE-Audio [9]\nUniFlow-Audio [30]\nDasheng AudioGen [8]\n\n✓\n△\n–\n✓\n–\n✓\n✓\n✓\n✓\n\n△\n✓\n✓\n✓\n✓\n✓\n–\n✓\n✓\n\n△\n△\n✓\n✓\n✓\n△\n✓\n✓\n✓\n\n–\n△\n–\n△\n–\n△\n–\n△\n✓\n\n–\n△\n△\n–", "numLines": 400, "startLine": 100, "totalLines": 2219}}}
