{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-822300a0fa9f048a", "type": "tool_result", "content": "500\t–\n501\t–\n502\t–\n503\t–\n504\t–\n505\t\n506\t△\n507\t–\n508\t–\n509\t–\n510\t–\n511\t✓\n512\t–\n513\t△\n514\t△\n515\t\n516\t–\n517\t–\n518\t–\n519\t–\n520\t–\n521\t△\n522\t–\n523\t△\n524\t△\n525\t\n526\tGeneral / compositional\n527\t\n528\tSonicWeave (ours)\n529\t\n530\t✓\n531\t\n532\t✓\n533\t\n534\t✓\n535\t\n536\t✓\n537\t\n538\t✓\n539\t\n540\t✓\n541\t\n542\t✓\n543\t\n544\tLoad Balancing.\n545\t\n546\tdiffusion phase. The gate is trained by the generation objective\n547\tas a conflict-conditioned evidence-reliance coefficient, not as a\n548\tcalibrated probability of evidence correctness.\n549\tThe meaning of g j acts directly in the expert-logit space:\n550\tthe prior and evidence routers first produce logits of the same\n551\tshape, and g j linearly interpolates between them:\n552\tℓ j = (1 − g j )ℓprior + g j ℓevid\n553\tj , P j = softmax(ℓ j ).\n554\t\n555\tTo prevent expert collapse we adopt a Switch-Transformerstyle auxiliary loss [15], computed at chunk granularity and\n556\tonly over valid chunks:\n557\tNe\n558\t\n559\tLMoE = Ne · ∑ p̄valid\n560\t· d̄valid\n561\t,\n562\ti\n563\ti\n564\t\n565\t(11)\n566\t\n567\twhere p̄valid is the mean router probability over valid chunks\n568\tand d̄valid is the mean top-K-normalised dispatch fraction.\n569\tPadded chunks are excluded from both statistics. The layerwise auxiliary losses are summed across the M CPE-MoE\n570\tlayers and weighted in the composite objective below.\n571\t\n572\tThus, g j = 0 exactly recovers the prior logits, whereas g j = 1\n573\texactly recovers the evidence logits. The top-K entries of P j\n574\tselect the experts, and their renormalized probabilities directly\n575\tweight the expert outputs.\n576\t\n577\t3.5\n578\tSparse Dispatch and Composition.\n579\t\n580\tConditional Flow-Matching Training\n581\t\n582\tGiven the ground-truth stereo-VAE latent x1 , we sample\n583\tx0 ∼ N (0, I) and t ∼ σ (N (0, 1)), form xt =(1−t)x0 +tx1 , and\n584\tregress the ground-truth velocity:\n585\t\n586\tTop-K indices and weights are broadcast to frames within each\n587\tchunk; active expert outputs are weighted and accumulated,\n588\twith padding masked out.\n589\tThe audio output of the layer is an equal-weight combination\n590\tof the shared expert and the sparse mixture,\n591\toaud = 12 MLPshared (haud ) + 12 osparse .\n592\t\n593\t(13)\n594\t\n595\ti=1\n596\t\n597\t2\n598\t\n599\tLFM = E vθ (xt , c, ctext ,t) − (x1 − x0 ) .\n600\t\n601\t(14)\n602\t\n603\tDuring training, we randomly mask a contiguous span of\n604\tthe audio reference and compute the reconstruction loss on the\n605\tmasked region; the audio reference and caption are independently dropped for classifier-free guidance [32].\n606\tThe composite objective is\n607\t\n608\t(12)\n609\t\n610\tThe down-projection of each routed expert is zero-initialized,\n611\tso the sparse contribution starts near zero and is introduced\n612\tsmoothly during optimization. The fixed 12 : 12 composition prevents an additional learnable mixture gate from suppressing the\n613\trouted path; gradients can update the expert down-projections\n614\tfrom the first step, after which the experts and router co-adapt.\n615\tThe final layer output is [octx ∥ oaud ].\n616\t\n617\tL = LFM + αCL LCL + αMoE LMoE .\n618\t\n619\t3.6\n620\t\n621\t(15)\n622\t\n623\tInference\n624\t\n625\tWe integrate the learned velocity field with a fixed-step Euler\n626\tODE solver, using 100 uniform steps in t ∈ [0, 1]; an optional\n627\t5\n628\t\n629\t\fMetrics.\n630\t\n631\tcosine sway sampling schedule accelerates inference at low\n632\tstep counts.\n633\tAt inference we adopt Adaptive Projected Guidance\n634\t(APG) [33] and adapt it to the clean-sample estimate induced\n635\tby the CFM velocity parameterisation. With conditional and unconditional clean-sample estimates µ̂ and µ̂∅ , let ∆µ = µ̂ − µ̂∅\n636\tand decompose it into components parallel and orthogonal to\n637\tµ̂, denoted by ∆µ∥ and ∆µ⊥ . The guided estimate is\n638\t\u0001\n639\tµ̂APG = µ̂∅ + w η ∆µ∥ + ∆µ⊥ ,\n640\t(16)\n641\t\n642\tFor all public benchmarks, we report objective metrics suited\n643\tto each task: WER/CER for TTS; FAD, KL divergence, and\n644\tCLAP for TTA; and KL divergence and CLAP for TTM. The\n645\tTTA and TTM KL/CLAP scores follow the Stable Audio metrics protocol with task-specific checkpoints, while TTA FAD\n646\tuses the AVBench FDVGG implementation. For the ComplexScene evaluation, we use Gemini-based reference-free judging\n647\ttogether with human listening tests. The two are used jointly\n648\tbecause the reference-free judge provides scalable coverage\n649\tover all prompts, while human MOS offers an independent perceptual assessment on a fixed subset. Gemini assigns 1-5 scores\n650\tfor semantic adherence and technical quality from the same\n651\tprompt; the concise scoring rubric is given in the appendix.\n652\t\n653\twhere w is the guidance scale and η damps the parallel component. It is then converted back to velocity space for ODE\n654\tintegration. Momentum smoothing, the clean-sample conversion, and the complete update are given in the appendix. The\n655\tfinal clean stereo latent is decoded into waveform audio by the\n656\tpretrained stereo VAE decoder.\n657\t\n658\t4\n659\t\n660\tExperiments\n661\t\n662\t4.1\n663\t\n664\tExperimental Setup\n665\t\n666\tImplementation Details.\n667\tThe CPE-MoE module replaces the dense FFN in the last 4\n668\tlayers, with 4 routed experts and top-2 selection at chunk size\n669\tC=4. Inference uses a fixed-step Euler ODE solver with APG\n670\tguidance. Training uses roughly five million 10–15-second\n671\tclips sampled from an internal corpus exceeding 20,000 hours,\n672\tin which speech, music, sound effects, ambience, and mixed\n673\trecordings coexist in a shared data distribution. Additional\n674\tmodel and optimization details, together with targeted studies\n675\tof gate adaptivity and chunk-size sensitivity, are provided in\n676\tthe appendix.\n677\t\n678\tTasks and Benchmarks.\n679\tWe evaluate one unified model on three public generation tasks\n680\tand a controlled complex-scene set. For text-to-speech (TTS),\n681\twe use SeedTTS-eval [34] and LibriSpeech-PC test-clean [35];\n682\tfor text-to-audio (TTA), we use AudioCaps [36]; for text-tomusic (TTM), we use MusicCaps [37] and Song Describer [38].\n683\tThe Complex-Scene set is described in the subsequent section.\n684\tFor SonicWeave and controlled baselines, we deterministically render benchmark inputs into a fixed schema using\n685\tconstrained field-extraction rules. Where automatic parsing\n686\tis needed, a zero-temperature language model acts only as a\n687\tschema-aware extractor and validator; it is prohibited from introducing information absent from the original input, apart\n688\tfrom fixed template defaults applied uniformly across the\n689\tbenchmark. The converter does not observe reference audio.\n690\t\n691\t4.2\n692\t\n693\tResults on Public Benchmarks\n694\t\n695\tTables 2-4 summarize the public-benchmark results. On TTS,\n696\tSonicWeave achieves the best or tied-best result on all three\n697\tsubsets, tying the strongest reported results on SeedTTS-eval\n698\tand LibriSpeech. Relative to the controlled baselines, it improves over Dense by 1.4, 1.0, and 2.5 percentage points on\n699\tthese subsets, and over Base-MoE by 0.4, 0.3, and 0.8 points,\n700\trespectively.\n701\tTable 2: TTS results on SeedTTS and LibriSpeech.\n702\t\n703\tBaselines.\n704\tWe compare against representative specialized and general\n705\taudio generators selected for task coverage, availability, and\n706\trecency. For Complex-Scene evaluation, we retain Higgs Audio\n707\tV2, UniFlow-Audio, and Dasheng AudioGen, as they provide\n708\tthe most relevant publicly available coverage of both speech\n709\tgeneration and general audio generation for mixed scenes.\n710\tTwo key ablation models, Dense (1B) and Base-MoE (2B,\n711\tmatching SonicWeave), share SonicWeave’s data and backbone. Dense replaces CPE-MoE with dense FFNs; Base-MoE\n712\tuses token-level top-K audio routing without conflict-gated\n713\tprior–evidence fusion. The Dense comparison measures the\n714\tbenefit of conditional capacity, whereas the parameter-matched\n715\tBase-MoE comparison isolates the effect of the routing design\n716\tmore directly.\n717\tThe complete model capability comparison is given in Table 1, while task-specific quantitative comparisons are reported\n718\tin Tables 2–4.\n719\t\n720\tModel\n721\t\n722\tSeedTTS-en SeedTTS-zh LibriSpeech\n723\tWER↓\n724\t\n725\tCER↓\n726\t\n727\tWER↓\n728\t\n729\tF5-TTS\n730\tCosyVoice 2\n731\tUniAudio\n732\tQwen2.5-Omni\n733\tUniMoE-Audio\n734\tHiggs Audio V2\n735\tStep-Audio\n736\t\n737\t1.9%\n738\t2.0%\n739\t7.2%\n740\t2.1%\n741\t1.3%\n742\t1.0%\n743\t2.2%\n744\t\n745\t1.6%\n746\t1.5%\n747\t–\n748\t1.6%\n749\t0.8%\n750\t0.8%\n751\t1.0%\n752\t\n753\t2.4%\n754\t2.5%\n755\t18.3%\n756\t7.6%\n757\t3.4%\n758\t3.6%\n759\t5.0%\n760\t\n761\tDense (ours)\n762\tBase-MoE (ours)\n763\tSonicWeave (ours)\n764\t\n765\t2.4%\n766\t1.4%\n767\t1.0%\n768\t\n769\t1.8%\n770\t1.1%\n771\t0.8%\n772\t\n773\t4.9%\n774\t3.2%\n775\t2.4%\n776\t\n777\tOn TTA, SonicWeave substantially improves over Dense\n778\tand Base-MoE in all three metrics. Its CLAP score reaches\n779\t0.475, close to the strongest reported baseline (0.480), while\n780\t6\n781\t\n782\t\fits KL is 1.26 and its FAD is 2.75. SonicWeave does not obtain\n783\tthe best value on every individual AudioCaps metric, but it narrows the gap to the strongest CLAP system while substantially\n784\timproving FAD and KL over the matched controls.\n785\t\n786\treceive the natural-language prompt in their native input format, whereas SonicWeave, Dense and Base-MoE receive the\n787\tcorresponding structured caption containing the same semantic\n788\tspecifications.\n789\t\n790\tTable 3: TTA results on AudioCaps.\n791\t\n792\tTable 5: Reference-free and human listening evaluation on the\n793\tComplex-Scene suite. AI-Tech and AI-Sem denote technical quality and semantic adherence, respectively; MOS-Q measures perceived\n794\taudio quality, while MOS-R measures whether the requested prompt\n795\tevents are jointly realized.\n796\t\n797\tModel\n798\t\n799\tFAD↓\n800\t\n801\tKL↓\n802\t\n803\tCLAP↑\n804\t\n805\tAudioLDM 2\n806\tTangoFlux\n807\tStable Audio Open\n808\tDasheng AudioGen\n809\tUniAudio\n810\tUniFlow-Audio\n811\tAudioX\n812\t\n813\t2.29\n814\t2.26\n815\t4.13\n816\t3.19\n817\t6.64\n818\t5.74\n819\t2.45\n820\t\n821\t1.41\n822\t1.15\n823\t2.14\n824\t1.86\n825\t–\n826\t1.43\n827\t1.27\n828\t\n829\t0.419\n830\t0.480\n831\t0.350\n832\t0.438\n833\t0.243\n834\t0.476\n835\t0.440\n836\t\n837\tDense (ours)\n838\tBase-MoE (ours)\n839\tSonicWeave (ours)\n840\t\n841\t3.98\n842\t3.10\n843\t2.75\n844\t\n845\t1.62\n846\t1.40\n847\t1.26\n848\t\n849\t0.389\n850\t0.422\n851\t0.475\n852\t\n853\tModel\n854\t\n855\tTable 4: TTM results on MusicCaps and Song Describer.\n856\t\n857\tMusicCaps\n858\t\n859\tSong Describer\n860\t\n861\tCLAP↑\n862\t\n863\tKL↓\n864\t\n865\tCLAP↑\n866\t\n867\tKL↓\n868\t\n869\tAudioLDM 2\n870\tMusicGen-Large\n871\tStable Audio Open\n872\tAudioX\n873\tDasheng AudioGen\n874\tUniMoE-Audio\n875\tUniFlow-Audio\n876\t\n877\t0.301\n878\t0.280\n879\t0.300\n880\t0.240\n881\t0.334\n882\t0.340\n883\t0.241\n884\t\n885\t1.20\n886\t1.31\n887\t1.44\n888\t0.96\n889\t1.37\n890\t1.25\n891\t1.87\n892\t\n893\t–\n894\t0.19\n895\t0.32\n896\t–\n897\t–\n898\t0.22\n899\t0.15\n900\t\n901\t–\n902\t0.54\n903\t0.62\n904\t–\n905\t–\n906\t–\n907\t–\n908\t\n909\tDense (ours)\n910\tBase-MoE (ours)\n911\tSonicWeave (ours)\n912\t\n913\t0.251\n914\t0.296\n915\t0.312\n916\t\n917\t1.31\n918\t1.10\n919\t1.06\n920\t\n921\t0.29\n922\t0.32\n923\t0.36\n924\t\n925\t0.58\n926\t0.48\n927\t0.44\n928\t\n929\t4.3\n930\t\n931\tHiggs Audio V2\n932\tUniFlow-Audio\n933\tDasheng AudioGen\n934\t\n935\t4.32\n936\t2.28\n937\t3.10\n938\t\n939\t3.54\n940\t1.77\n941\t3.07\n942\t\n943\t3.92±0.21 2.42±0.64\n944\t1.99±0.33 1.87±0.80\n945\t2.97±0.41 3.25±0.73\n946\t\n947\tDense (ours)\n948\tBase-MoE (ours)\n949\tSonicWeave (ours)\n950\t\n951\t4.13\n952\t4.62\n953\t4.79\n954\t\n955\t4.23\n956\t4.58\n957\t4.72\n958\t\n959\t4.29±0.24 4.15±0.37\n960\t4.57±0.13 4.31±0.31\n961\t4.57±0.19 4.49±0.38\n962\t\n963\tTable 5 shows consistent trends between the reference-free\n964\tAI evaluation and human listening tests. SonicWeave achieves\n965\tthe highest AI semantic-adherence and technical-quality scores\n966\tand the highest human MOS-R. Higgs Audio V2 retains relatively competitive perceived quality but receives a much lower\n967\trequest-realization score, showing that audio quality alone\n968\tdoes not imply faithful realization of all requested scene components. Dasheng AudioGen and UniFlow-Audio additionally\n969\tshow less consistent performance across the heterogeneous\n970\tprompts. SonicWeave and Base-MoE are effectively tied on\n971\tMOS-Q, whereas SonicWeave is consistently stronger in semantic adherence and request realization. This supports the\n972\tintended role of CPE-MoE: it preserves the acoustic quality\n973\tof the underlying generator while improving compositional\n974\tfidelity and fine-grained control in complex scenes.\n975\t\n976\tOn TTM, SonicWeave achieves the lowest KL and highest\n977\tCLAP on Song Describer among the listed systems. On MusicCaps, it achieves a competitive KL of 1.06 and CLAP of 0.312,\n978\toffering a strong quality–alignment trade-off and placing it on\n979\tthe Pareto frontier among the listed baselines. Compared with\n980\tDense and Base-MoE, SonicWeave consistently improves both\n981\tKL and CLAP across the two datasets, showing that CPE-MoE\n982\tstrengthens text–music correspondence without uniformly surpassing every specialized baseline.\n983\t\n984\tModel\n985\t\n986\tAI-Tech↑ AI-Sem↑ MOS-Q↑ MOS-R↑\n987\t\n988\t4.4\n989\t\n990\tRouting Analysis and Visualization\n991\t\n992\tTo explain the behavior of SonicWeave beyond aggregate generation metrics, we analyze the routing patterns induced by\n993\tits CPE-MoE module. The main-text visualization focuses\n994\ton population-level evidence: content-conditioned expert usage and the evolution of the conflict gate across diffusion\n995\tphases. Representative mixed-scene traces are reserved for the\n996\tappendix because they illustrate the mechanism qualitatively\n997\trather than providing a statistically aggregated result.\n998\tFor this analysis, we construct a 250-prompt routing benchmark by sampling examples from several single-task evaluation\n999\tsets and combining them with complex-scene prompts. We\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "99f9f2f5-463f-4d73-8c1b-6ee352cfb6b2", "uuid": "58e8ff26-3314-4a02-9794-464258c58218", "timestamp": "2026-08-11T14:44:30.507Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/sonicweave.txt", "content": "–\n–\n–\n–\n–\n\n△\n–\n–\n–\n–\n✓\n–\n△\n△\n\n–\n–\n–\n–\n–\n△\n–\n△\n△\n\nGeneral / compositional\n\nSonicWeave (ours)\n\n✓\n\n✓\n\n✓\n\n✓\n\n✓\n\n✓\n\n✓\n\nLoad Balancing.\n\ndiffusion phase. The gate is trained by the generation objective\nas a conflict-conditioned evidence-reliance coefficient, not as a\ncalibrated probability of evidence correctness.\nThe meaning of g j acts directly in the expert-logit space:\nthe prior and evidence routers first produce logits of the same\nshape, and g j linearly interpolates between them:\nℓ j = (1 − g j )ℓprior + g j ℓevid\nj , P j = softmax(ℓ j ).\n\nTo prevent expert collapse we adopt a Switch-Transformerstyle auxiliary loss [15], computed at chunk granularity and\nonly over valid chunks:\nNe\n\nLMoE = Ne · ∑ p̄valid\n· d̄valid\n,\ni\ni\n\n(11)\n\nwhere p̄valid is the mean router probability over valid chunks\nand d̄valid is the mean top-K-normalised dispatch fraction.\nPadded chunks are excluded from both statistics. The layerwise auxiliary losses are summed across the M CPE-MoE\nlayers and weighted in the composite objective below.\n\nThus, g j = 0 exactly recovers the prior logits, whereas g j = 1\nexactly recovers the evidence logits. The top-K entries of P j\nselect the experts, and their renormalized probabilities directly\nweight the expert outputs.\n\n3.5\nSparse Dispatch and Composition.\n\nConditional Flow-Matching Training\n\nGiven the ground-truth stereo-VAE latent x1 , we sample\nx0 ∼ N (0, I) and t ∼ σ (N (0, 1)), form xt =(1−t)x0 +tx1 , and\nregress the ground-truth velocity:\n\nTop-K indices and weights are broadcast to frames within each\nchunk; active expert outputs are weighted and accumulated,\nwith padding masked out.\nThe audio output of the layer is an equal-weight combination\nof the shared expert and the sparse mixture,\noaud = 12 MLPshared (haud ) + 12 osparse .\n\n(13)\n\ni=1\n\n2\n\nLFM = E vθ (xt , c, ctext ,t) − (x1 − x0 ) .\n\n(14)\n\nDuring training, we randomly mask a contiguous span of\nthe audio reference and compute the reconstruction loss on the\nmasked region; the audio reference and caption are independently dropped for classifier-free guidance [32].\nThe composite objective is\n\n(12)\n\nThe down-projection of each routed expert is zero-initialized,\nso the sparse contribution starts near zero and is introduced\nsmoothly during optimization. The fixed 12 : 12 composition prevents an additional learnable mixture gate from suppressing the\nrouted path; gradients can update the expert down-projections\nfrom the first step, after which the experts and router co-adapt.\nThe final layer output is [octx ∥ oaud ].\n\nL = LFM + αCL LCL + αMoE LMoE .\n\n3.6\n\n(15)\n\nInference\n\nWe integrate the learned velocity field with a fixed-step Euler\nODE solver, using 100 uniform steps in t ∈ [0, 1]; an optional\n5\n\n\fMetrics.\n\ncosine sway sampling schedule accelerates inference at low\nstep counts.\nAt inference we adopt Adaptive Projected Guidance\n(APG) [33] and adapt it to the clean-sample estimate induced\nby the CFM velocity parameterisation. With conditional and unconditional clean-sample estimates µ̂ and µ̂∅ , let ∆µ = µ̂ − µ̂∅\nand decompose it into components parallel and orthogonal to\nµ̂, denoted by ∆µ∥ and ∆µ⊥ . The guided estimate is\n\u0001\nµ̂APG = µ̂∅ + w η ∆µ∥ + ∆µ⊥ ,\n(16)\n\nFor all public benchmarks, we report objective metrics suited\nto each task: WER/CER for TTS; FAD, KL divergence, and\nCLAP for TTA; and KL divergence and CLAP for TTM. The\nTTA and TTM KL/CLAP scores follow the Stable Audio metrics protocol with task-specific checkpoints, while TTA FAD\nuses the AVBench FDVGG implementation. For the ComplexScene evaluation, we use Gemini-based reference-free judging\ntogether with human listening tests. The two are used jointly\nbecause the reference-free judge provides scalable coverage\nover all prompts, while human MOS offers an independent perceptual assessment on a fixed subset. Gemini assigns 1-5 scores\nfor semantic adherence and technical quality from the same\nprompt; the concise scoring rubric is given in the appendix.\n\nwhere w is the guidance scale and η damps the parallel component. It is then converted back to velocity space for ODE\nintegration. Momentum smoothing, the clean-sample conversion, and the complete update are given in the appendix. The\nfinal clean stereo latent is decoded into waveform audio by the\npretrained stereo VAE decoder.\n\n4\n\nExperiments\n\n4.1\n\nExperimental Setup\n\nImplementation Details.\nThe CPE-MoE module replaces the dense FFN in the last 4\nlayers, with 4 routed experts and top-2 selection at chunk size\nC=4. Inference uses a fixed-step Euler ODE solver with APG\nguidance. Training uses roughly five million 10–15-second\nclips sampled from an internal corpus exceeding 20,000 hours,\nin which speech, music, sound effects, ambience, and mixed\nrecordings coexist in a shared data distribution. Additional\nmodel and optimization details, together with targeted studies\nof gate adaptivity and chunk-size sensitivity, are provided in\nthe appendix.\n\nTasks and Benchmarks.\nWe evaluate one unified model on three public generation tasks\nand a controlled complex-scene set. For text-to-speech (TTS),\nwe use SeedTTS-eval [34] and LibriSpeech-PC test-clean [35];\nfor text-to-audio (TTA), we use AudioCaps [36]; for text-tomusic (TTM), we use MusicCaps [37] and Song Describer [38].\nThe Complex-Scene set is described in the subsequent section.\nFor SonicWeave and controlled baselines, we deterministically render benchmark inputs into a fixed schema using\nconstrained field-extraction rules. Where automatic parsing\nis needed, a zero-temperature language model acts only as a\nschema-aware extractor and validator; it is prohibited from introducing information absent from the original input, apart\nfrom fixed template defaults applied uniformly across the\nbenchmark. The converter does not observe reference audio.\n\n4.2\n\nResults on Public Benchmarks\n\nTables 2-4 summarize the public-benchmark results. On TTS,\nSonicWeave achieves the best or tied-best result on all three\nsubsets, tying the strongest reported results on SeedTTS-eval\nand LibriSpeech. Relative to the controlled baselines, it improves over Dense by 1.4, 1.0, and 2.5 percentage points on\nthese subsets, and over Base-MoE by 0.4, 0.3, and 0.8 points,\nrespectively.\nTable 2: TTS results on SeedTTS and LibriSpeech.\n\nBaselines.\nWe compare against representative specialized and general\naudio generators selected for task coverage, availability, and\nrecency. For Complex-Scene evaluation, we retain Higgs Audio\nV2, UniFlow-Audio, and Dasheng AudioGen, as they provide\nthe most relevant publicly available coverage of both speech\ngeneration and general audio generation for mixed scenes.\nTwo key ablation models, Dense (1B) and Base-MoE (2B,\nmatching SonicWeave), share SonicWeave’s data and backbone. Dense replaces CPE-MoE with dense FFNs; Base-MoE\nuses token-level top-K audio routing without conflict-gated\nprior–evidence fusion. The Dense comparison measures the\nbenefit of conditional capacity, whereas the parameter-matched\nBase-MoE comparison isolates the effect of the routing design\nmore directly.\nThe complete model capability comparison is given in Table 1, while task-specific quantitative comparisons are reported\nin Tables 2–4.\n\nModel\n\nSeedTTS-en SeedTTS-zh LibriSpeech\nWER↓\n\nCER↓\n\nWER↓\n\nF5-TTS\nCosyVoice 2\nUniAudio\nQwen2.5-Omni\nUniMoE-Audio\nHiggs Audio V2\nStep-Audio\n\n1.9%\n2.0%\n7.2%\n2.1%\n1.3%\n1.0%\n2.2%\n\n1.6%\n1.5%\n–\n1.6%\n0.8%\n0.8%\n1.0%\n\n2.4%\n2.5%\n18.3%\n7.6%\n3.4%\n3.6%\n5.0%\n\nDense (ours)\nBase-MoE (ours)\nSonicWeave (ours)\n\n2.4%\n1.4%\n1.0%\n\n1.8%\n1.1%\n0.8%\n\n4.9%\n3.2%\n2.4%\n\nOn TTA, SonicWeave substantially improves over Dense\nand Base-MoE in all three metrics. Its CLAP score reaches\n0.475, close to the strongest reported baseline (0.480), while\n6\n\n\fits KL is 1.26 and its FAD is 2.75. SonicWeave does not obtain\nthe best value on every individual AudioCaps metric, but it narrows the gap to the strongest CLAP system while substantially\nimproving FAD and KL over the matched controls.\n\nreceive the natural-language prompt in their native input format, whereas SonicWeave, Dense and Base-MoE receive the\ncorresponding structured caption containing the same semantic\nspecifications.\n\nTable 3: TTA results on AudioCaps.\n\nTable 5: Reference-free and human listening evaluation on the\nComplex-Scene suite. AI-Tech and AI-Sem denote technical quality and semantic adherence, respectively; MOS-Q measures perceived\naudio quality, while MOS-R measures whether the requested prompt\nevents are jointly realized.\n\nModel\n\nFAD↓\n\nKL↓\n\nCLAP↑\n\nAudioLDM 2\nTangoFlux\nStable Audio Open\nDasheng AudioGen\nUniAudio\nUniFlow-Audio\nAudioX\n\n2.29\n2.26\n4.13\n3.19\n6.64\n5.74\n2.45\n\n1.41\n1.15\n2.14\n1.86\n–\n1.43\n1.27\n\n0.419\n0.480\n0.350\n0.438\n0.243\n0.476\n0.440\n\nDense (ours)\nBase-MoE (ours)\nSonicWeave (ours)\n\n3.98\n3.10\n2.75\n\n1.62\n1.40\n1.26\n\n0.389\n0.422\n0.475\n\nModel\n\nTable 4: TTM results on MusicCaps and Song Describer.\n\nMusicCaps\n\nSong Describer\n\nCLAP↑\n\nKL↓\n\nCLAP↑\n\nKL↓\n\nAudioLDM 2\nMusicGen-Large\nStable Audio Open\nAudioX\nDasheng AudioGen\nUniMoE-Audio\nUniFlow-Audio\n\n0.301\n0.280\n0.300\n0.240\n0.334\n0.340\n0.241\n\n1.20\n1.31\n1.44\n0.96\n1.37\n1.25\n1.87\n\n–\n0.19\n0.32\n–\n–\n0.22\n0.15\n\n–\n0.54\n0.62\n–\n–\n–\n–\n\nDense (ours)\nBase-MoE (ours)\nSonicWeave (ours)\n\n0.251\n0.296\n0.312\n\n1.31\n1.10\n1.06\n\n0.29\n0.32\n0.36\n\n0.58\n0.48\n0.44\n\n4.3\n\nHiggs Audio V2\nUniFlow-Audio\nDasheng AudioGen\n\n4.32\n2.28\n3.10\n\n3.54\n1.77\n3.07\n\n3.92±0.21 2.42±0.64\n1.99±0.33 1.87±0.80\n2.97±0.41 3.25±0.73\n\nDense (ours)\nBase-MoE (ours)\nSonicWeave (ours)\n\n4.13\n4.62\n4.79\n\n4.23\n4.58\n4.72\n\n4.29±0.24 4.15±0.37\n4.57±0.13 4.31±0.31\n4.57±0.19 4.49±0.38\n\nTable 5 shows consistent trends between the reference-free\nAI evaluation and human listening tests. SonicWeave achieves\nthe highest AI semantic-adherence and technical-quality scores\nand the highest human MOS-R. Higgs Audio V2 retains relatively competitive perceived quality but receives a much lower\nrequest-realization score, showing that audio quality alone\ndoes not imply faithful realization of all requested scene components. Dasheng AudioGen and UniFlow-Audio additionally\nshow less consistent performance across the heterogeneous\nprompts. SonicWeave and Base-MoE are effectively tied on\nMOS-Q, whereas SonicWeave is consistently stronger in semantic adherence and request realization. This supports the\nintended role of CPE-MoE: it preserves the acoustic quality\nof the underlying generator while improving compositional\nfidelity and fine-grained control in complex scenes.\n\nOn TTM, SonicWeave achieves the lowest KL and highest\nCLAP on Song Describer among the listed systems. On MusicCaps, it achieves a competitive KL of 1.06 and CLAP of 0.312,\noffering a strong quality–alignment trade-off and placing it on\nthe Pareto frontier among the listed baselines. Compared with\nDense and Base-MoE, SonicWeave consistently improves both\nKL and CLAP across the two datasets, showing that CPE-MoE\nstrengthens text–music correspondence without uniformly surpassing every specialized baseline.\n\nModel\n\nAI-Tech↑ AI-Sem↑ MOS-Q↑ MOS-R↑\n\n4.4\n\nRouting Analysis and Visualization\n\nTo explain the behavior of SonicWeave beyond aggregate generation metrics, we analyze the routing patterns induced by\nits CPE-MoE module. The main-text visualization focuses\non population-level evidence: content-conditioned expert usage and the evolution of the conflict gate across diffusion\nphases. Representative mixed-scene traces are reserved for the\nappendix because they illustrate the mechanism qualitatively\nrather than providing a statistically aggregated result.\nFor this analysis, we construct a 250-prompt routing benchmark by sampling examples from several single-task evaluation\nsets and combining them with complex-scene prompts. We", "numLines": 500, "startLine": 500, "totalLines": 2219}}}
