{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b295d243423c0516", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1000\tgroup the resulting prompts into speech, music, singing, SFX,\n1001\tand mixed-scene categories, and aggregate routing statistics\n1002\tover all valid chunks and routed layers.\n1003\t\n1004\tComplex-Scene Evaluation\n1005\t\n1006\tPublic benchmarks mainly evaluate isolated speech, music, or\n1007\tenvironmental sounds and therefore underrepresent the heterogeneous compositions targeted by SonicWeave. We construct a controlled suite of 100 manually curated complexscene prompts, comprising 45 speech-with-background cases,\n1008\t10 singing-with-background cases, 15 clean dialogues, and\n1009\t30 dialogues with background. Each case is provided in both\n1010\tnatural-language and structured-caption forms. Public systems\n1011\t\n1012\tContent-conditioned expert specialization.\n1013\tFigure 3(a) shows the mean dispatch fraction for each expert\n1014\tacross the four CPE-MoE layers and five content groups. The\n1015\tload is clearly non-uniform and content dependent: speech\n1016\t7\n1017\t\n1018\t\f(a)\n1019\t\n1020\t(b)\n1021\t\n1022\tFigure 3: Routing analysis on the balanced routing benchmark. (a) Content-conditioned expert dispatch across routed layers, showing\n1023\tnon-uniform and content-dependent specialization. (b) Mean conflict gate E[g j |t] across diffusion phases, showing content-dependent and\n1024\tphase-dependent prior–evidence balancing.\n1025\t\n1026\tand SFX favor different expert combinations from music\n1027\tand singing, while mixed scenes retain a distinct distribution\n1028\trather than collapsing to one of the single-content patterns.\n1029\tThis specialization is also layer dependent. For example, music and singing place more mass on different experts in the\n1030\tdeeper routed layers, whereas speech consistently emphasizes\n1031\ta smaller subset of experts. These observations indicate that\n1032\tthe MoE is using conditional capacity to form acoustic specializations, rather than merely distributing tokens uniformly\n1033\tacross experts.\n1034\t\n1035\tconditioning pathway for text and time tokens.\n1036\tAcross public TTS, text-to-audio, and text-to-music benchmarks, SonicWeave consistently improves over matched Dense\n1037\tand token-routed MoE controls. On the Complex-Scene suite,\n1038\tthe principal gain is compositional: SonicWeave improves semantic adherence and joint request realization while maintaining perceptual quality comparable to the parameter-matched\n1039\tBase-MoE. Quantitatively, it reduces TTA FAD by 30.9% over\n1040\tDense and improves Complex-Scene MOS-R by 0.18 over\n1041\tBase-MoE and by more than 1.2 points over the strongest external baseline. The learned expert assignments and conflict-gate\n1042\ttrajectories also vary with content, layer depth, and diffusion\n1043\tphase, providing evidence that the additional capacity is used\n1044\tconditionally rather than uniformly.\n1045\tTargeted studies reported in the appendix further compare\n1046\tthe learned gate with fixed prior–evidence averaging and vary\n1047\tthe routing chunk size. Fixed fusion degrades all reported task\n1048\tgroups, while increasing the chunk size from 4 to 8 weakens\n1049\tmost metrics, supporting adaptive fusion and relatively finegrained chunk routing.\n1050\tThese findings support chunk-level prior–evidence routing\n1051\tas a useful intermediate granularity for unified audio generation.\n1052\tAt the same time, the current evaluation is limited to shortform generation, a two-speaker structured interface, and nonexhaustive routing ablations. Extending the approach to longer\n1053\tscenes, richer speaker interactions, and explicit temporal plans\n1054\tremains an important direction.\n1055\t\n1056\tPhase-dependent conflict gating.\n1057\tFigure 3(b) reports E[g j |t] for each content group. The curves\n1058\tare content dependent and non-monotonic: the gate changes\n1059\trapidly during early structure formation, reaches contentspecific operating ranges, and gradually returns toward a more\n1060\tbalanced regime during late acoustic refinement. Speech and\n1061\tSFX maintain higher evidence reliance than music and singing\n1062\tover much of the trajectory, while mixed scenes lie between\n1063\tthese regimes. Thus, the model does not apply a single fixed\n1064\tprior–evidence interpolation to all audio. Instead, it adjusts\n1065\tevidence reliance as a function of both acoustic content and\n1066\tdiffusion phase, consistent with the intended role of the conflict\n1067\tgate.\n1068\t\n1069\t5\n1070\t\n1071\tConclusion\n1072\tReferences\n1073\t\n1074\tThis paper presented SonicWeave, a unified flow-matching\n1075\tmodel for generating speech, singing, music, sound effects,\n1076\tand their mixtures with a single set of parameters. The central\n1077\tpremise of this work is that conditional computation in unified\n1078\taudio must operate at two coupled scales: it must accommodate\n1079\tdifferences across acoustic domains while adapting to heterogeneous regions within the same scene. CPE-MoE addresses this\n1080\trequirement by routing temporally contiguous audio chunks\n1081\tthrough a learned combination of a global text-and-diffusion\n1082\tprior and local acoustic evidence, while preserving a shared\n1083\t\n1084\t[1] Yaron Lipman, Ricky T. Q. Chen, Heli Ben-Hamu, Maximilian Nickel, and Matthew Le. Flow matching for generative modeling. In The Eleventh International Conference\n1085\ton Learning Representations, pages 1–28, 2023.\n1086\t[2] Matthew Le, Apoorv Vyas, Bowen Shi, Brian Karrer,\n1087\tLeda Sari, Rashel Moritz, Mary Williamson, Vimal\n1088\tManohar, Yossi Adi, Jay Mahadeokar, et al. Voicebox:\n1089\tText-guided multilingual universal speech generation at\n1090\t8\n1091\t\n1092\t\fscale. Advances in neural information processing systems,\n1093\t36:14005–14034, 2023.\n1094\t\n1095\t[10] Haohe Liu, Zehua Chen, Yi Yuan, Xinhao Mei, Xubo Liu,\n1096\tDanilo P. Mandic, Wenwu Wang, and Mark D. Plumbley.\n1097\tAudioLDM: Text-to-audio generation with latent diffusion models. In Proceedings of the 40th International\n1098\tConference on Machine Learning, pages 21450–21474,\n1099\t2023.\n1100\t\n1101\t[3] Yushen Chen, Zhikang Niu, Ziyang Ma, Keqi Deng,\n1102\tChunhui Wang, Jian Zhao, Kai Yu, and Xie Chen. F5TTS: A fairytaler that fakes fluent and faithful speech\n1103\twith flow matching. In Proceedings of the 63rd Annual\n1104\tMeeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics\n1105\t(Volume 1: Long Papers), pages 6255–6271, 2025.\n1106\t\n1107\t[11] Chunyu Qiang, Xiaopeng Wang, Kang Yin, Yuzhe Liang,\n1108\tYuxin Guo, Teng Ma, Ziyu Zhang, Tianrui Wang, Cheng\n1109\tGong, Yushen Chen, Ruibo Fu, Longbiao Wang, and\n1110\tJianwu Dang. UniSonate: A unified model for speech,\n1111\tmusic, and sound effect generation with text instructions.\n1112\tIn Proceedings of the 64th Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics, pages 28043–28054,\n1113\t2026.\n1114\t\n1115\t[4] Chia-Yu Hung, Navonil Majumder, Zhifeng Kong, Ambuj\n1116\tMehrish, Amir Zadeh, Chuan Li, Rafael Valle, Bryan\n1117\tCatanzaro, and Soujanya Poria. Tangoflux: Super fast and\n1118\tfaithful text to audio generation with flow matching and\n1119\tclap-ranked preference optimization. In The Fourteenth\n1120\tInternational Conference on Learning Representations,\n1121\tpages 1–24, 2026.\n1122\t\n1123\t[12] Jiawei Huang, Yi Ren, Rongjie Huang, Dongchao\n1124\tYang, Zhenhui Ye, Chen Zhang, Jinglin Liu, Xiang\n1125\tYin, Zejun Ma, and Zhou Zhao. Make-An-Audio 2:\n1126\tTemporal-enhanced text-to-audio generation. CoRR,\n1127\tabs/2305.18474, 2023. doi: 10.48550/arXiv.2305.18474.\n1128\t\n1129\t[5] Haohe Liu, Yi Yuan, Xubo Liu, Xinhao Mei, Qiuqiang\n1130\tKong, Qiao Tian, Yuping Wang, Wenwu Wang, Yuxuan\n1131\tWang, and Mark D. Plumbley. AudioLDM 2: Learning\n1132\tholistic audio generation with self-supervised pretraining. IEEE/ACM Transactions on Audio, Speech, and\n1133\tLanguage Processing, 32:2871–2883, 2024.\n1134\t\n1135\t[13] Deepanway Ghosal, Navonil Majumder, Ambuj Mehrish,\n1136\tand Soujanya Poria. Text-to-audio generation using instruction guided latent diffusion model. In Proceedings\n1137\tof the 31st ACM international conference on multimedia,\n1138\tpages 3590–3598, 2023.\n1139\t\n1140\t[6] Dongchao Yang, Jinchuan Tian, Xu Tan, Rongjie Huang,\n1141\tSongxiang Liu, Haohan Guo, Xuankai Chang, Jiatong\n1142\tShi, Sheng Zhao, Jiang Bian, Zhou Zhao, Xixin Wu, and\n1143\tHelen M. Meng. UniAudio: Towards universal audio\n1144\tgeneration with large language models. In Proceedings of\n1145\tthe 41st International Conference on Machine Learning,\n1146\tpages 56422–56447, 2024.\n1147\t\n1148\t[14] Yuxuan Jiang, Zehua Chen, Zeqian Ju, Chang Li, Weibei\n1149\tDou, and Jun Zhu. Freeaudio: Training-free timing planning for controllable long-form text-to-audio generation.\n1150\tIn Proceedings of the 33rd ACM International Conference on Multimedia, pages 9871–9880, 2025.\n1151\t\n1152\t[7] Apoorv Vyas, Bowen Shi, Matthew Le, Andros Tjandra,\n1153\tYi-Chiao Wu, Baishan Guo, Jiemin Zhang, Xinyue Zhang,\n1154\tRobert Adkins, William Ngan, Jeff Wang, Ivan Cruz, Bapi\n1155\tAkula, Akinniyi Akinyemi, Brian Ellis, Rashel Moritz,\n1156\tYael Yungster, Alice Rakotoarison, Liang Tan, Chris Summers, Carleigh Wood, Joshua Lane, Mary Williamson,\n1157\tand Wei-Ning Hsu. Audiobox: Unified audio generation\n1158\twith natural language prompts. CoRR, abs/2312.15821,\n1159\t2023. doi: 10.48550/arXiv.2312.15821.\n1160\t\n1161\t[15] William Fedus, Barret Zoph, and Noam Shazeer. Switch\n1162\ttransformers: Scaling to trillion parameter models with\n1163\tsimple and efficient sparsity. Journal of Machine Learning Research, 23(120):1–39, 2022.\n1164\t[16] Zhengcong Fei, Mingyuan Fan, Changqian Yu, Debang\n1165\tLi, and Junshi Huang. Scaling diffusion transformers to\n1166\t1