{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-be3a58e09e545373", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1500\t\n1501\ttext\n1502\tcategorical\n1503\t\n1504\trelation\n1505\t\n1506\tshort phrase\n1507\t\n1508\tvocal\n1509\t\n1510\tshort phrase\n1511\t\n1512\tmusic\n1513\t\n1514\tshort phrase\n1515\t\n1516\tsfx\n1517\t\n1518\tshort phrase\n1519\t\n1520\tambience\n1521\t\n1522\tshort phrase\n1523\t\n1524\ttexture\n1525\t\n1526\tshort phrase\n1527\t\n1528\tDominant audio mode: speech, singing, music, sound effects, or\n1529\tmixed. mixed is used only when two or more of speech, singing,\n1530\tmusic, and sound effects are explicitly requested; it does not itself\n1531\timply speech.\n1532\tLanguage of intelligible speech or singing. It is omitted for nonvocal audio.\n1533\tSpoken linguistic content, including ordered dialogue turns separated by slashes when applicable.\n1534\tSong lyrics content when provided.\n1535\tNumber of foreground speakers or vocalists when applicable: 1 or\n1536\t2. Dialogue scenes support at most two speakers.\n1537\tSpeaker roles, turn-taking relation, or foreground interaction, such\n1538\tas a host and guest or two commuters.\n1539\tSpeaker identity descriptors, vocal timbre, delivery, emotion, accent, or recording coloration relevant to the foreground voice.\n1540\tInstrumentation, genre, tempo, mood, and relative role of music in\n1541\tthe scene.\n1542\tDiscrete non-musical events, such as a door slam, train brakes,\n1543\tkeyboard taps, or rain impacts.\n1544\tPersistent environment and room-scale context, such as an indoor\n1545\tstation, kitchen room tone, or open coastline.\n1546\tMix and recording relationships: foreground prominence, masking,\n1547\treverberation, stereo impression, or dynamic behavior.\n1548\t\n1549\ttions, but to normalize diverse descriptions into a fixed set of\n1550\tfields that can be processed consistently by the text conditioning module. The structured representation covers acoustic type,\n1551\tlanguage, speech content or lyrics, speaker-related attributes,\n1552\tmusic, sound events, ambience, and recording texture. Thus,\n1553\t“rule-based templates” refers to the fixed conversion rules and\n1554\toutput schema, rather than unconstrained caption rewriting.\n1555\tWe formulate the conversion as constrained information extraction. Given a natural-language annotation, the converter\n1556\tretains the core summary supplied by the source annotation in\n1557\tthe summary field and maps the remaining explicitly stated information into the predefined structured fields. Quoted speech\n1558\tand lyrics are copied verbatim when available, while unsupported fields remain empty. The converter is prohibited from\n1559\tintroducing new events, speaker identities, emotions, acoustic\n1560\tproperties, or stylistic attributes that are not stated in the source\n1561\tdescription.\n1562\tThe conversion follows a fixed schema and deterministic\n1563\tfield constraints. When automatic conversion is required, we\n1564\timplement the constrained extractor with a language model\n1565\t(Qwen3.5-35B-A3B with temperature set to 0.0) as a schemaaware mapper and validator: it applies the predefined rules,\n1566\tformats the output into the required structure, and checks for\n1567\tinvalid or unsupported fields. It does not perform free-form caption generation. This design allows both training annotations\n1568\tand inference prompts to share the same structured interface\n1569\twhile preserving the semantics of the original natural-language\n1570\tinput.\n1571\t\n1572\tThe complete conversion instruction used by the extractor is\n1573\tprovided below.\n1574\tNatural-to-Structured Conversion Prompt\n1575\tYou are a structured audio metadata extractor for a text-to-audio\n1576\tmodel.\n1577\tYou receive a natural-language description of an audio clip. Your\n1578\ttask is to convert it into a JSON object following the exact schema\n1579\tprovided below.\n1580\tSTRICT RULES:\n1581\t1. Output valid JSON only. Do not include markdown, explanations, or extra text.\n1582\t2. Use only the predefined field names in the schema. Do not add\n1583\tnew keys.\n1584\t3. summary:\n1585\t- Copy the source-provided core summary verbatim.\n1586\t- Do not paraphrase, expand, or rewrite it.\n1587\t- If the input contains separate summary and detailed-description\n1588\tfields, use only the designated summary field.\n1589\t- If no explicit core summary is provided, copy the original naturallanguage description verbatim; do not generate a new summary.\n1590\t4. speech:\n1591\t- If spoken words are explicitly quoted, copy them exactly.\n1592\t- Otherwise leave it empty.\n1593\t- Do not create speech content for clips without human speech.\n1594\t5. lyrics: fill only when song lyrics are explicitly provided. Otherwise leave it empty.\n1595\t6. Select exactly one type:\n1596\tspeech: dominant human speech;\n1597\t\n1598\t13\n1599\t\n1600\t\fB.4\n1601\t\n1602\tsinging: dominant singing or vocal performance;\n1603\tmusic: non-vocal instrumental music;\n1604\tsfx: discrete sound events such as impacts, machines, animals, or\n1605\tweather;\n1606\tmixed: two or more foreground acoustic categories among speech,\n1607\tsinging, music, and sound effects coexist and are jointly relevant;\n1608\tunknown: insufficient information.\n1609\t7. language:\n1610\t- Use an ISO language code only when human speech or singing\n1611\tis present.\n1612\t- Otherwise leave it empty.\n1613\t8. sfx should describe specific discrete events rather than generic\n1614\tlabels.\n1615\t9. ambience describes persistent environmental background.\n1616\t10. texture describes recording or acoustic characteristics only.\n1617\t11. vocal describe voice characteristics only.\n1618\t12. speaker_count must be one of: \"1\", \"2\", \"unknown\", or \"\".\n1619\t13. music should describe only explicitly supported musical information, including instruments, rhythm, style, or mood when\n1620\tavailable.\n1621\t14. Do not infer missing information from world knowledge or\n1622\tcontext. All unsupported fields must remain empty. All field values should be concise noun phrases rather than complete sentences.\n1623\tJSON Schema (output exactly this structure):\n1624\t{ \"summary\": \"\", \"type\": \"\", \"lang\": \"\", \"speech\": \"\", \"lyrics\": \"\",\n1625\t\"speaker_count\": \"\", \"relation\": \"\", \"vocal\": \"\", \"music\": \"\", \"sfx\":\n1626\t\"\", \"ambience\": \"\", \"texture\": \"\" }\n1627\t\n1628\tThe following templates illustrate the interface used during\n1629\ttraining and inference. Bracketed fields are optional and omitted when unsupported. A mixed scene explicitly contains at\n1630\tleast two of speech, music, singing, and sound effects.\n1631\tSpeech:\n1632\tsummary <type>speech</type>\n1633\t<lang>LANG</lang> <speech>TEXT</speech>\n1634\t<speaker_count>N</speaker_count>\n1635\t<vocal>VOICE</vocal> [<ambience>ROOM</ambience>]\n1636\t[<texture>MIX</texture>]\n1637\tMusic: summary <type>music</type> <music>INSTRUMENTS,\n1638\tSTYLE, TEMPO</music> [<texture>PRODUCTION</texture>]\n1639\tSinging:\n1640\tsummary <type>singing</type>\n1641\t<lang>LANG</lang> <lyrics>LYRICS</lyrics>\n1642\t<vocal>VOCAL STYLE</vocal>\n1643\t<music>ACCOMPANIMENT</music>\n1644\t[<texture>MIX</texture>]\n1645\tSFX: summary <type>sfx</type> <sfx>EVENTS</sfx>\n1646\t<ambience>ENVIRONMENT</ambience> [<texture>RECORDING\n1647\tCHARACTER</texture>]\n1648\tMixed\n1649\tscene:\n1650\tsummary <type>mixed</type>\n1651\t<lang>LANG</lang> <speech>TEXT</speech>\n1652\t<speaker_count>N</speaker_count>\n1653\t<vocal>VOICE</vocal> [<music>BED</music>]\n1654\t[<sfx>EVENTS</sfx>] [<ambience>ENVIRONMENT</ambience>]\n1655\t<texture>FOREGROUND–BACKGROUND RELATION</texture>\n1656\t\n1657\tB.5\n1658\tThe extracted JSON representation is deterministically serialized into the XML-like tagged text format consumed by the\n1659\ttext encoder; this serialization changes only the representation\n1660\tformat and does not add semantic information.\n1661\t\n1662\tB.3\n1663\t\n1664\tPrompt Templates for Unified Audio Scenes\n1665\t\n1666\tRepresentative Structured Prompts\n1667\t\n1668\tTable 8 illustrates how the renderer preserves source information. For TTS, the original speech content is placed directly in\n1669\tspeech without adding scene content. For TTA and TTM, the\n1670\toriginal caption is retained verbatim as the first summary sentence, with the remaining fields only reorganizing information\n1671\talready present in that caption. The two complex-scene rows\n1672\tshow how speech or lyrics, music, sound effects, ambience, and\n1673\ttheir foreground–background relation are represented together.\n1674\t\n1675\tPublic-Benchmark Prompt Rendering\n1676\t\n1677\tFor public benchmarks, the structured representation is used as\n1678\tan interface normalization step rather than an additional source\n1679\tof information. The original benchmark input remains the only\n1680\tsemantic source, and the conversion procedure does not access\n1681\treference audio or introduce additional acoustic descriptions.\n1682\tFor TTS benchmarks, transcripts are preserved exactly in the\n1683\tspeech field. The converter only adds schema-level information\n1684\tsuch as the acoustic type, language identifier, and available\n1685\tspeaker metadata. No words, pronunciation details, or speaker\n1686\tcharacteristics are generated beyond the original input.\n1687\tFor caption-based benchmarks, including AudioCaps, MusicCaps, and Song Describer, the original caption is retained\n1688\tas the summary field. The structured converter maps the caption into the predefined fields and performs schema validation.\n1689\tIn particular, speech and lyrics are preserved verbatim when\n1690\texplicitly present, while events, instruments, attributes, or emotions not supported by the source caption are not introduced.\n1691\tThe same structured interface is used for training and inference, but the benchmark conversion is applied only to the\n1692\tpublicly available text descriptions. Since the conversion does\n1693\tnot observe the target audio, the resulting structured caption\n1694\tcontains no information unavailable from the original benchmark input.\n1695\t\n1696\tC\n1697\t\n1698\tTraining and Inference Details\n1699\t\n1700\tC.1\n1701\t\n1702\tTraining Setup\n1703\t\n1704\tThe reported model is trained on approximately five million\n1705\tclips of 10–15 seconds from an internal corpus exceeding\n1706\t20,000 hours. The corpus combines speech, singing, music,\n1707\tsound effects, and mixed recordings under the unified prompt\n1708\tinterface. The approximate content distribution is 20% speech,\n1709\t5% singing, 10% music, 10% sound effects, and 55% mixed\n1710\trecordings. We use the same data distribution, DiT backbone,\n1711\ttext encoder, and training budget for Dense, Base-MoE, and\n1712\tSonicWeave; only the FFN/routing mechanism differs in the\n1713\tcontrolled comparison.\n1714\tThe final training run used AdamW with a peak learning\n1715\trate of 7.5 × 10−5 . The learning-rate schedule consists of the\n1716\tconfigured linear warm-up followed by linear decay. Training\n1717\tused fp16 mixed precision, with a global batch size of 512 and\n1718\tone gradient-accumulation step, for 800,000 optimizer updates.\n1719\tThe trainer maintained an exponential moving average with\n1720\t14\n1721\t\n1722\t\fTable 8: Representative prompts. The examples illustrate the field semantics; they do not define a required set of fields for every sample.\n1723\t\n1724\tScenario\n1725\t\n1726\tStructured prompt\n1727\t\n1728\tTTS\n1729\t\n1730\tplain English speech <type>speech</type> <lang>en</lang>\n1731\t<speech>Keep walking along this road, then turn right at the second\n1732\tintersection.</speech> <speaker_count>1</speaker_count> <vocal>adult\n1733\tEnglish voice, neutral tone</vocal>\n1734\t\n1735\tTTA\n1736\t\n1737\tA train arrives while people talk on a platform <type>sfx</type>\n1738\t<sfx>train arriving, people talking</sfx> <ambience>train station\n1739\tplatform with crowd murmur</ambience>\n1740\t\n1741\tTTM\n1742\t\n1743\tWarm instrumental piano with slow chords <type>music</type>\n1744\t<music>instrumental piano, slow warm chords</music> <texture>close studio\n1745\trecording</texture>\n1746\t\n1747\tMixed\n1748\t\n1749\tEnglish race-engineer and driver radio dialogue at high speed\n1750\t<type>mixed</type> <lang>en</lang> <speech>Box this lap; the rear\n1751\ttemperatures are climbing. / Copy, but the steering is vibrating\n1752\tunder braking. / Understood, stay off the inside curb.</speech>\n1753\t<speaker_count>2</speaker_count> <relation>pit engineer advising a race\n1754\tdriver</relation> <vocal>controlled male engineer and strained male\n1755\tdriver through clipped team radio</vocal> <sfx>Engine roar, wind, tires,\n1756\tand radio pops collide</sfx> <ambience>race car at speed with strong\n1757\twind trackside</ambience> <texture>driver transmission is noisier and\n1758\tmore distorted than the engineer response</texture>\n1759\t\n1760\tWe define ∆µ = µ c − µ u and apply reverse momentum,\n1761\t\n1762\tdecay 0.9999 after a 1,000-step warm-up. We selected the\n1763\tfinal checkpoint for evaluation and used its EMA parameters\n1764\twhen available; no intermediate checkpoint was selected by a\n1765\tvalidation-score sweep.\n1766\t\n1767\tf = ∆µ + β ∆µ\n1768\tf ,\n1769\t∆µ\n1770\tk\n1771\tk\n1772\tk−1\n1773\t\n1774\t∆µ ∥ =\n1775\t\n1776\tFor each training sample, a contiguous span whose fractional\n1777\tlength is sampled uniformly from [0.7, 1.0] is selected from\n1778\tvalid latent frames. This region is zeroed in the reference condition but remains visible in the clean target and flow objective.\n1779\tThe mechanism trains continuation and inpainting behavior\n1780\twithout changing the flow target.\n1781\tClassifier-free guidance conditions are sampled as follows.\n1782\tThe audio reference is independently dropped with probability\n1783\t0.3 and the caption with probability 0.1. Caption drop zeros\n1784\tboth text tokens and the pooled global text condition. When\n1785\taudio reference is retained, LFM is restricted to the selected\n1786\tmasked span; when it is dropped, the loss is evaluated over\n1787\tall valid frames. This makes the null branch a genuine textto-audio training condition rather than a masked-reference\n1788\treconstruction condition.\n1789\t\n1790\tf − ∆µ .\n1791\t∆µ ⊥ = ∆µ\n1792\t∥\n1793\t\n1794\t\u0010\n1795\t\u0011\n1796\tµ APG = µ u + w ∆µ ⊥ + η∆µ ∥ ,\n1797\t\n1798\t(21)\n1799\t\n1800\t(22)\n1801\t\n1802\tand is converted back to velocity space as\n1803\tvAPG =\n1804\t\n1805\tµ APG − xt\n1806\t.\n1807\tmax(1 − t, 10−5 )\n1808\t\n1809\t(23)\n1810\t\n1811\twhere w is the guidance scale. We use η = 0.85 for speechonly generation and η = 0.5 when the requested scene contains\n1812\tsound effects or music; the latter setting gave the best results\n1813\tfor these mixed conditions. APG is disabled when w < 10−5 .\n1814\tThe unconditional pass uses dropped caption and dropped audio reference; for masked-reference editing, the noisy-state\n1815\tmasking policy is applied consistently to that pass [33].\n1816\t\n1817\tAt ODE state xt , let vc and vu denote the conditional and\n1818\tunconditional velocity predictions evaluated at the same state.\n1819\tUnder the linear flow path, their clean-sample estimates are\n1820\tµ u = xt + (1 − t)vu .\n1821\t\n1822\tf µ ⟩\n1823\t⟨∆µ,\n1824\tc\n1825\tµ ,\n1826\t∥µ c ∥22 + ε c\n1827\t\n1828\tThe guided clean estimate is\n1829\t\n1830\tAdaptive Projected Guidance\n1831\t\n1832\tµ c = xt + (1 − t)vc ,\n1833\t\n1834\t(20)\n1835\t\n1836\tf = ∆µ . We decompose the smoothed guidance direcwith ∆µ\n1837\t0\n1838\t0\n1839\ttion with respect to the conditional clean estimate:\n1840\t\n1841\tC.2 Conditioning Dropout and Reference Masking\n1842\t\n1843\tC.3\n1844\t\n1845\tβ = −0.1,\n1846\t\n1847\tC.4\n1848\t\n1849\tSampling Protocol\n1850\t\n1851\tUnless a task-specific protocol states otherwise, SonicWeave\n1852\tuses Euler integration over 100 uniform time points from 0 to 1,\n1853\t\n1854\t(19)\n1855\t15\n1856\t\n1857\t\fD.2\n1858\t\n1859\tfixed random seed 42, no reference audio, and full-span latent\n1860\tgeneration. The parallel inference entry point uses guidance\n1861\tscale 4.5 for the reported generation path. The model supports a\n1862\tcosine sway schedule at lower sampling budgets, but the publicbenchmark and Complex-Scene results should be generated\n1863\twith the fixed protocol above rather than selecting a schedule\n1864\tper example.\n1865\tThe audio waveform sample rate is 44.1 kHz, and the stereo\n1866\tVAE has a temporal downsampling factor of 2048 samples\n1867\tper latent frame. We use a pretrained stereo VAE based on the\n1868\tStable Audio 2.0 VAE architecture [27]. Public-benchmark decoding uses the duration requested by the corresponding benchmark protocol: generated latent length is set from the benchmark item or its task-specific maximum rather than silently\n1869\ttruncating all tasks to one common duration. The model defaults to generate 256 latent frames, corresponding to approximately 11.89s at 44.1 kHz. Because the flow-matching DiT\n1870\toperates on a variable-length latent sequence, it is not architecturally restricted to this default duration.\n1871\t\n1872\tD\n1873\t\n1874\tEvaluation Protocols\n1875\t\n1876\tD.1\n1877\t\n1878\tPublic-Benchmark Protocols\n1879\t\n1880\tComplex-Scene Suite\n1881\t\n1882\tThe Complex-Scene suite contains 100 prompts across four\n1883\tcategories: 45 speech-with-background cases, 10 singing-withbackground cases, 15 clean dialogue cases, and 30 dialoguewith-background cases. It tests not only whether individual\n1884\tcomponents occur, but whether linguistic content, speaker\n1885\tstructure, music, sound effects, ambience, and foreground–\n1886\tbackground relationships are jointly realized. The suite includes Mandarin and English prompts, with clean dialogue\n1887\tserving as a control condition and the remaining categories\n1888\temphasizing coexistence, masking, and composition.\n1889\tEach case has a natural-language prompt and a paired structured prompt. The natural-language form specifies the complete\n1890\ttarget scene in ordinary prose. The structured form reorganizes the same specification into the fields of Table 7; it does\n1891\tnot add new target events. Public systems receive the naturallanguage form in their native interface. SonicWeave, Dense,\n1892\tand Base-MoE receive the paired structured form. This setup\n1893\tevaluates structured conditioning and CPE-MoE under the\n1894\tsame requested semantics while avoiding unsupported claims\n1895\tof identical tokenizer-level inputs across unrelated public systems.\n1896\tRepresentative cases are listed in Table 8. They include\n1897\tspeech–background, singing–background, clean dialogue, and\n1898\tdialogue–background conditions, with both continuous background layers and short high-salience events. The suite therefore tests whether a system preserves foreground speech or\n1899\tsinging while realizing local acoustic departures and their intended foreground–background relation.\n1900\t\n1901\tWe evaluate text-to-speech (TTS) on SeedTTS-eval and\n1902\tLibriSpeech-PC test-clean, text-to-audio (TTA) on AudioCaps,\n1903\tand text-to-music (TTM) on MusicCaps and Song Describer.\n1904\tFor TTS, we report word error rate (WER) on English benchmarks and character error rate (CER) on Mandarin benchmarks. TTA is evaluated using VGGish-based Fr’echet distance (FDVGG , also referred to as FAD), PaSST-based KL\n1905\tdivergence, and CLAP text–audio similarity. TTM is evaluated using PaSST-based KL divergence and CLAP similarity. Lower WER/CER, FDVGG , and KL indicate better performance, whereas higher CLAP is better.\n1906\tEach system generates one audio sample per evaluation item\n1907\tunder a common fixed-seed policy. WER/CER is computed between the benchmark transcript and an ASR transcription of the\n1908\tgenerated audio after applying the official evaluation normalization, including punctuation removal and English lowercasing.\n1909\tCLAP is computed as the mean cosine similarity between the\n1910\ttext-prompt and generated-audio embeddings. FDVGG and KL\n1911\tare aggregate metrics and are reported once for each benchmark set. The number of retained items is obtained from the\n1912\tfinal evaluation manifest for each benchmark.\n1913\tFor TTS transcription, we follow the public SeedTTS/F5TTS evaluation pipeline, using faster-Whisper large-v3 [40]\n1914\tfor English and FunASR Paraformer-zh [41] for Mandarin. For AudioCaps, FDVGG is computed using the default VGGish-based implementation in AVBench. PaSST KL\n1915\tfollows the Stable Audio Metrics implementation. CLAP\n1916\tis evaluated with LAION-CLAP, using the general-audio\n1917\tcheckpoint 630k-audioset-fusion-best.pt for TTA and\n1918\tmusic_speech_audioset_epoch_15_esc_89.98.pt for TTM.\n1919\t\n1920\tD.3\n1921\t\n1922\tReference-Free Judge Rubric\n1923\t\n1924\tWe use gemini-3.1-pro-preview as a blind audio judge\n1925\twhen a reference recording is unavailable. The judge receives\n1926\tone generated audio clip, the natural-language prompt, and the\n1927\tpaired structured specification. Its instruction is: “Listen to the\n1928\taudio first; score only audible evidence against the requested\n1929\tscene; do not credit an event solely because it appears in the\n1930\tprompt; return the required JSON fields.” The system/model\n1931\tname is not supplied.\n1932\tEach applicable dimension is scored on a five-point ordinal\n1933\tscale represented internally as 1–5: 1 denotes absent, contradictory, or completely wrong; 2 mostly wrong with limited\n1934\trelevant evidence; 3 partially correct with substantial omissions or errors; 4 mostly correct with minor omissions; and 5\n1935\tfully and accurately realized. The seven dimensions are:\n1936\t• Speech content: required words and language;\n1937\t• Vocal attributes: speaker type, emotion, energy, delivery,\n1938\tand style;\n1939\t• Speaker structure: count, identity separation, turns, and\n1940\tassignment;\n1941\t• Background adherence: requested music, events, and\n1942\tambience;\n1943\t16\n1944\t\n1945\t\fTable 9: Targeted routing ablations on representative benchmarks. All variants share the same backbone, training data, expert configuration, and\n1946\ttraining budget. Complex-scene results use the same reference-free automatic judging protocol as the main evaluation.\n1947\t\n1948\tVariant\n1949\t\n1950\tSeedTTS\n1951\t\n1952\tAudioCaps\n1953\t\n1954\tSong Describer\n1955\t\n1956\tEn WER↓ Zh CER↓ FAD↓ KL↓ CLAP↑ KL↓\n1957\tFixed gate (g j = 0.5, C = 4)\n1958\tCPE-MoE (C = 8)\n1959\tCPE-MoE (C = 4)\n1960\t\n1961\t2.1%\n1962\t1.4%\n1963\t1.0%\n1964\t\n1965\t1.8%\n1966\t0.9%\n1967\t0.8%\n1968\t\n1969\t3.69\n1970\t2.91\n1971\t2.75\n1972\t\n1973\t• Acoustic relation: prominence, masking, timing, overlap,\n1974\tand interaction;\n1975\t\n1976\t0.448\n1977\t0.469\n1978\t0.475\n1979\t\n1980\t0.53\n1981\t0.44\n1982\t0.36\n1983\t\n1984\t0.31\n1985\t0.33\n1986\t0.44\n1987\t\n1988\tAI-Tech↑ AI-Sem↑\n1989\t4.60\n1990\t4.69\n1991\t4.79\n1992\t\n1993\t4.35\n1994\t4.64\n1995\t4.72\n1996\t\n1997\tanonymously shuffled system outputs per case, for 150 clips\n1998\tand 300 ordinal ratings (MOS-Q and MOS-R) per completed\n1999\tresponse. The study script randomizes clip order within each\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "99f9f2f5-463f-4d73-8c1b-6ee352cfb6b2", "uuid": "3e801ffb-19e7-459e-9ad8-b02a2cee6641", "timestamp": "2026-08-11T14:44:44.674Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/sonicweave.txt", "content": "\ntext\ncategorical\n\nrelation\n\nshort phrase\n\nvocal\n\nshort phrase\n\nmusic\n\nshort phrase\n\nsfx\n\nshort phrase\n\nambience\n\nshort phrase\n\ntexture\n\nshort phrase\n\nDominant audio mode: speech, singing, music, sound effects, or\nmixed. mixed is used only when two or more of speech, singing,\nmusic, and sound effects are explicitly requested; it does not itself\nimply speech.\nLanguage of intelligible speech or singing. It is omitted for nonvocal audio.\nSpoken linguistic content, including ordered dialogue turns separated by slashes when applicable.\nSong lyrics content when provided.\nNumber of foreground speakers or vocalists when applicable: 1 or\n2. Dialogue scenes support at most two speakers.\nSpeaker roles, turn-taking relation, or foreground interaction, such\nas a host and guest or two commuters.\nSpeaker identity descriptors, vocal timbre, delivery, emotion, accent, or recording coloration relevant to the foreground voice.\nInstrumentation, genre, tempo, mood, and relative role of music in\nthe scene.\nDiscrete non-musical events, such as a door slam, train brakes,\nkeyboard taps, or rain impacts.\nPersistent environment and room-scale context, such as an indoor\nstation, kitchen room tone, or open coastline.\nMix and recording relationships: foreground prominence, masking,\nreverberation, stereo impression, or dynamic behavior.\n\ntions, but to normalize diverse descriptions into a fixed set of\nfields that can be processed consistently by the text conditioning module. The structured representation covers acoustic type,\nlanguage, speech content or lyrics, speaker-related attributes,\nmusic, sound events, ambience, and recording texture. Thus,\n“rule-based templates” refers to the fixed conversion rules and\noutput schema, rather than unconstrained caption rewriting.\nWe formulate the conversion as constrained information extraction. Given a natural-language annotation, the converter\nretains the core summary supplied by the source annotation in\nthe summary field and maps the remaining explicitly stated information into the predefined structured fields. Quoted speech\nand lyrics are copied verbatim when available, while unsupported fields remain empty. The converter is prohibited from\nintroducing new events, speaker identities, emotions, acoustic\nproperties, or stylistic attributes that are not stated in the source\ndescription.\nThe conversion follows a fixed schema and deterministic\nfield constraints. When automatic conversion is required, we\nimplement the constrained extractor with a language model\n(Qwen3.5-35B-A3B with temperature set to 0.0) as a schemaaware mapper and validator: it applies the predefined rules,\nformats the output into the required structure, and checks for\ninvalid or unsupported fields. It does not perform free-form caption generation. This design allows both training annotations\nand inference prompts to share the same structured interface\nwhile preserving the semantics of the original natural-language\ninput.\n\nThe complete conversion instruction used by the extractor is\nprovided below.\nNatural-to-Structured Conversion Prompt\nYou are a structured audio metadata extractor for a text-to-audio\nmodel.\nYou receive a natural-language description of an audio clip. Your\ntask is to convert it into a JSON object following the exact schema\nprovided below.\nSTRICT RULES:\n1. Output valid JSON only. Do not include markdown, explanations, or extra text.\n2. Use only the predefined field names in the schema. Do not add\nnew keys.\n3. summary:\n- Copy the source-provided core summary verbatim.\n- Do not paraphrase, expand, or rewrite it.\n- If the input contains separate summary and detailed-description\nfields, use only the designated summary field.\n- If no explicit core summary is provided, copy the original naturallanguage description verbatim; do not generate a new summary.\n4. speech:\n- If spoken words are explicitly quoted, copy them exactly.\n- Otherwise leave it empty.\n- Do not create speech content for clips without human speech.\n5. lyrics: fill only when song lyrics are explicitly provided. Otherwise leave it empty.\n6. Select exactly one type:\nspeech: dominant human speech;\n\n13\n\n\fB.4\n\nsinging: dominant singing or vocal performance;\nmusic: non-vocal instrumental music;\nsfx: discrete sound events such as impacts, machines, animals, or\nweather;\nmixed: two or more foreground acoustic categories among speech,\nsinging, music, and sound effects coexist and are jointly relevant;\nunknown: insufficient information.\n7. language:\n- Use an ISO language code only when human speech or singing\nis present.\n- Otherwise leave it empty.\n8. sfx should describe specific discrete events rather than generic\nlabels.\n9. ambience describes persistent environmental background.\n10. texture describes recording or acoustic characteristics only.\n11. vocal describe voice characteristics only.\n12. speaker_count must be one of: \"1\", \"2\", \"unknown\", or \"\".\n13. music should describe only explicitly supported musical information, including instruments, rhythm, style, or mood when\navailable.\n14. Do not infer missing information from world knowledge or\ncontext. All unsupported fields must remain empty. All field values should be concise noun phrases rather than complete sentences.\nJSON Schema (output exactly this structure):\n{ \"summary\": \"\", \"type\": \"\", \"lang\": \"\", \"speech\": \"\", \"lyrics\": \"\",\n\"speaker_count\": \"\", \"relation\": \"\", \"vocal\": \"\", \"music\": \"\", \"sfx\":\n\"\", \"ambience\": \"\", \"texture\": \"\" }\n\nThe following templates illustrate the interface used during\ntraining and inference. Bracketed fields are optional and omitted when unsupported. A mixed scene explicitly contains at\nleast two of speech, music, singing, and sound effects.\nSpeech:\nsummary <type>speech</type>\n<lang>LANG</lang> <speech>TEXT</speech>\n<speaker_count>N</speaker_count>\n<vocal>VOICE</vocal> [<ambience>ROOM</ambience>]\n[<texture>MIX</texture>]\nMusic: summary <type>music</type> <music>INSTRUMENTS,\nSTYLE, TEMPO</music> [<texture>PRODUCTION</texture>]\nSinging:\nsummary <type>singing</type>\n<lang>LANG</lang> <lyrics>LYRICS</lyrics>\n<vocal>VOCAL STYLE</vocal>\n<music>ACCOMPANIMENT</music>\n[<texture>MIX</texture>]\nSFX: summary <type>sfx</type> <sfx>EVENTS</sfx>\n<ambience>ENVIRONMENT</ambience> [<texture>RECORDING\nCHARACTER</texture>]\nMixed\nscene:\nsummary <type>mixed</type>\n<lang>LANG</lang> <speech>TEXT</speech>\n<speaker_count>N</speaker_count>\n<vocal>VOICE</vocal> [<music>BED</music>]\n[<sfx>EVENTS</sfx>] [<ambience>ENVIRONMENT</ambience>]\n<texture>FOREGROUND–BACKGROUND RELATION</texture>\n\nB.5\nThe extracted JSON representation is deterministically serialized into the XML-like tagged text format consumed by the\ntext encoder; this serialization changes only the representation\nformat and does not add semantic information.\n\nB.3\n\nPrompt Templates for Unified Audio Scenes\n\nRepresentative Structured Prompts\n\nTable 8 illustrates how the renderer preserves source information. For TTS, the original speech content is placed directly in\nspeech without adding scene content. For TTA and TTM, the\noriginal caption is retained verbatim as the first summary sentence, with the remaining fields only reorganizing information\nalready present in that caption. The two complex-scene rows\nshow how speech or lyrics, music, sound effects, ambience, and\ntheir foreground–background relation are represented together.\n\nPublic-Benchmark Prompt Rendering\n\nFor public benchmarks, the structured representation is used as\nan interface normalization step rather than an additional source\nof information. The original benchmark input remains the only\nsemantic source, and the conversion procedure does not access\nreference audio or introduce additional acoustic descriptions.\nFor TTS benchmarks, transcripts are preserved exactly in the\nspeech field. The converter only adds schema-level information\nsuch as the acoustic type, language identifier, and available\nspeaker metadata. No words, pronunciation details, or speaker\ncharacteristics are generated beyond the original input.\nFor caption-based benchmarks, including AudioCaps, MusicCaps, and Song Describer, the original caption is retained\nas the summary field. The structured converter maps the caption into the predefined fields and performs schema validation.\nIn particular, speech and lyrics are preserved verbatim when\nexplicitly present, while events, instruments, attributes, or emotions not supported by the source caption are not introduced.\nThe same structured interface is used for training and inference, but the benchmark conversion is applied only to the\npublicly available text descriptions. Since the conversion does\nnot observe the target audio, the resulting structured caption\ncontains no information unavailable from the original benchmark input.\n\nC\n\nTraining and Inference Details\n\nC.1\n\nTraining Setup\n\nThe reported model is trained on approximately five million\nclips of 10–15 seconds from an internal corpus exceeding\n20,000 hours. The corpus combines speech, singing, music,\nsound effects, and mixed recordings under the unified prompt\ninterface. The approximate content distribution is 20% speech,\n5% singing, 10% music, 10% sound effects, and 55% mixed\nrecordings. We use the same data distribution, DiT backbone,\ntext encoder, and training budget for Dense, Base-MoE, and\nSonicWeave; only the FFN/routing mechanism differs in the\ncontrolled comparison.\nThe final training run used AdamW with a peak learning\nrate of 7.5 × 10−5 . The learning-rate schedule consists of the\nconfigured linear warm-up followed by linear decay. Training\nused fp16 mixed precision, with a global batch size of 512 and\none gradient-accumulation step, for 800,000 optimizer updates.\nThe trainer maintained an exponential moving average with\n14\n\n\fTable 8: Representative prompts. The examples illustrate the field semantics; they do not define a required set of fields for every sample.\n\nScenario\n\nStructured prompt\n\nTTS\n\nplain English speech <type>speech</type> <lang>en</lang>\n<speech>Keep walking along this road, then turn right at the second\nintersection.</speech> <speaker_count>1</speaker_count> <vocal>adult\nEnglish voice, neutral tone</vocal>\n\nTTA\n\nA train arrives while people talk on a platform <type>sfx</type>\n<sfx>train arriving, people talking</sfx> <ambience>train station\nplatform with crowd murmur</ambience>\n\nTTM\n\nWarm instrumental piano with slow chords <type>music</type>\n<music>instrumental piano, slow warm chords</music> <texture>close studio\nrecording</texture>\n\nMixed\n\nEnglish race-engineer and driver radio dialogue at high speed\n<type>mixed</type> <lang>en</lang> <speech>Box this lap; the rear\ntemperatures are climbing. / Copy, but the steering is vibrating\nunder braking. / Understood, stay off the inside curb.</speech>\n<speaker_count>2</speaker_count> <relation>pit engineer advising a race\ndriver</relation> <vocal>controlled male engineer and strained male\ndriver through clipped team radio</vocal> <sfx>Engine roar, wind, tires,\nand radio pops collide</sfx> <ambience>race car at speed with strong\nwind trackside</ambience> <texture>driver transmission is noisier and\nmore distorted than the engineer response</texture>\n\nWe define ∆µ = µ c − µ u and apply reverse momentum,\n\ndecay 0.9999 after a 1,000-step warm-up. We selected the\nfinal checkpoint for evaluation and used its EMA parameters\nwhen available; no intermediate checkpoint was selected by a\nvalidation-score sweep.\n\nf = ∆µ + β ∆µ\nf ,\n∆µ\nk\nk\nk−1\n\n∆µ ∥ =\n\nFor each training sample, a contiguous span whose fractional\nlength is sampled uniformly from [0.7, 1.0] is selected from\nvalid latent frames. This region is zeroed in the reference condition but remains visible in the clean target and flow objective.\nThe mechanism trains continuation and inpainting behavior\nwithout changing the flow target.\nClassifier-free guidance conditions are sampled as follows.\nThe audio reference is independently dropped with probability\n0.3 and the caption with probability 0.1. Caption drop zeros\nboth text tokens and the pooled global text condition. When\naudio reference is retained, LFM is restricted to the selected\nmasked span; when it is dropped, the loss is evaluated over\nall valid frames. This makes the null branch a genuine textto-audio training condition rather than a masked-reference\nreconstruction condition.\n\nf − ∆µ .\n∆µ ⊥ = ∆µ\n∥\n\n\u0010\n\u0011\nµ APG = µ u + w ∆µ ⊥ + η∆µ ∥ ,\n\n(21)\n\n(22)\n\nand is converted back to velocity space as\nvAPG =\n\nµ APG − xt\n.\nmax(1 − t, 10−5 )\n\n(23)\n\nwhere w is the guidance scale. We use η = 0.85 for speechonly generation and η = 0.5 when the requested scene contains\nsound effects or music; the latter setting gave the best results\nfor these mixed conditions. APG is disabled when w < 10−5 .\nThe unconditional pass uses dropped caption and dropped audio reference; for masked-reference editing, the noisy-state\nmasking policy is applied consistently to that pass [33].\n\nAt ODE state xt , let vc and vu denote the conditional and\nunconditional velocity predictions evaluated at the same state.\nUnder the linear flow path, their clean-sample estimates are\nµ u = xt + (1 − t)vu .\n\nf µ ⟩\n⟨∆µ,\nc\nµ ,\n∥µ c ∥22 + ε c\n\nThe guided clean estimate is\n\nAdaptive Projected Guidance\n\nµ c = xt + (1 − t)vc ,\n\n(20)\n\nf = ∆µ . We decompose the smoothed guidance direcwith ∆µ\n0\n0\ntion with respect to the conditional clean estimate:\n\nC.2 Conditioning Dropout and Reference Masking\n\nC.3\n\nβ = −0.1,\n\nC.4\n\nSampling Protocol\n\nUnless a task-specific protocol states otherwise, SonicWeave\nuses Euler integration over 100 uniform time points from 0 to 1,\n\n(19)\n15\n\n\fD.2\n\nfixed random seed 42, no reference audio, and full-span latent\ngeneration. The parallel inference entry point uses guidance\nscale 4.5 for the reported generation path. The model supports a\ncosine sway schedule at lower sampling budgets, but the publicbenchmark and Complex-Scene results should be generated\nwith the fixed protocol above rather than selecting a schedule\nper example.\nThe audio waveform sample rate is 44.1 kHz, and the stereo\nVAE has a temporal downsampling factor of 2048 samples\nper latent frame. We use a pretrained stereo VAE based on the\nStable Audio 2.0 VAE architecture [27]. Public-benchmark decoding uses the duration requested by the corresponding benchmark protocol: generated latent length is set from the benchmark item or its task-specific maximum rather than silently\ntruncating all tasks to one common duration. The model defaults to generate 256 latent frames, corresponding to approximately 11.89s at 44.1 kHz. Because the flow-matching DiT\noperates on a variable-length latent sequence, it is not architecturally restricted to this default duration.\n\nD\n\nEvaluation Protocols\n\nD.1\n\nPublic-Benchmark Protocols\n\nComplex-Scene Suite\n\nThe Complex-Scene suite contains 100 prompts across four\ncategories: 45 speech-with-background cases, 10 singing-withbackground cases, 15 clean dialogue cases, and 30 dialoguewith-background cases. It tests not only whether individual\ncomponents occur, but whether linguistic content, speaker\nstructure, music, sound effects, ambience, and foreground–\nbackground relationships are jointly realized. The suite includes Mandarin and English prompts, with clean dialogue\nserving as a control condition and the remaining categories\nemphasizing coexistence, masking, and composition.\nEach case has a natural-language prompt and a paired structured prompt. The natural-language form specifies the complete\ntarget scene in ordinary prose. The structured form reorganizes the same specification into the fields of Table 7; it does\nnot add new target events. Public systems receive the naturallanguage form in their native interface. SonicWeave, Dense,\nand Base-MoE receive the paired structured form. This setup\nevaluates structured conditioning and CPE-MoE under the\nsame requested semantics while avoiding unsupported claims\nof identical tokenizer-level inputs across unrelated public systems.\nRepresentative cases are listed in Table 8. They include\nspeech–background, singing–background, clean dialogue, and\ndialogue–background conditions, with both continuous background layers and short high-salience events. The suite therefore tests whether a system preserves foreground speech or\nsinging while realizing local acoustic departures and their intended foreground–background relation.\n\nWe evaluate text-to-speech (TTS) on SeedTTS-eval and\nLibriSpeech-PC test-clean, text-to-audio (TTA) on AudioCaps,\nand text-to-music (TTM) on MusicCaps and Song Describer.\nFor TTS, we report word error rate (WER) on English benchmarks and character error rate (CER) on Mandarin benchmarks. TTA is evaluated using VGGish-based Fr’echet distance (FDVGG , also referred to as FAD), PaSST-based KL\ndivergence, and CLAP text–audio similarity. TTM is evaluated using PaSST-based KL divergence and CLAP similarity. Lower WER/CER, FDVGG , and KL indicate better performance, whereas higher CLAP is better.\nEach system generates one audio sample per evaluation item\nunder a common fixed-seed policy. WER/CER is computed between the benchmark transcript and an ASR transcription of the\ngenerated audio after applying the official evaluation normalization, including punctuation removal and English lowercasing.\nCLAP is computed as the mean cosine similarity between the\ntext-prompt and generated-audio embeddings. FDVGG and KL\nare aggregate metrics and are reported once for each benchmark set. The number of retained items is obtained from the\nfinal evaluation manifest for each benchmark.\nFor TTS transcription, we follow the public SeedTTS/F5TTS evaluation pipeline, using faster-Whisper large-v3 [40]\nfor English and FunASR Paraformer-zh [41] for Mandarin. For AudioCaps, FDVGG is computed using the default VGGish-based implementation in AVBench. PaSST KL\nfollows the Stable Audio Metrics implementation. CLAP\nis evaluated with LAION-CLAP, using the general-audio\ncheckpoint 630k-audioset-fusion-best.pt for TTA and\nmusic_speech_audioset_epoch_15_esc_89.98.pt for TTM.\n\nD.3\n\nReference-Free Judge Rubric\n\nWe use gemini-3.1-pro-preview as a blind audio judge\nwhen a reference recording is unavailable. The judge receives\none generated audio clip, the natural-language prompt, and the\npaired structured specification. Its instruction is: “Listen to the\naudio first; score only audible evidence against the requested\nscene; do not credit an event solely because it appears in the\nprompt; return the required JSON fields.” The system/model\nname is not supplied.\nEach applicable dimension is scored on a five-point ordinal\nscale represented internally as 1–5: 1 denotes absent, contradictory, or completely wrong; 2 mostly wrong with limited\nrelevant evidence; 3 partially correct with substantial omissions or errors; 4 mostly correct with minor omissions; and 5\nfully and accurately realized. The seven dimensions are:\n• Speech content: required words and language;\n• Vocal attributes: speaker type, emotion, energy, delivery,\nand style;\n• Speaker structure: count, identity separation, turns, and\nassignment;\n• Background adherence: requested music, events, and\nambience;\n16\n\n\fTable 9: Targeted routing ablations on representative benchmarks. All variants share the same backbone, training data, expert configuration, and\ntraining budget. Complex-scene results use the same reference-free automatic judging protocol as the main evaluation.\n\nVariant\n\nSeedTTS\n\nAudioCaps\n\nSong Describer\n\nEn WER↓ Zh CER↓ FAD↓ KL↓ CLAP↑ KL↓\nFixed gate (g j = 0.5, C = 4)\nCPE-MoE (C = 8)\nCPE-MoE (C = 4)\n\n2.1%\n1.4%\n1.0%\n\n1.8%\n0.9%\n0.8%\n\n3.69\n2.91\n2.75\n\n• Acoustic relation: prominence, masking, timing, overlap,\nand interaction;\n\n0.448\n0.469\n0.475\n\n0.53\n0.44\n0.36\n\n0.31\n0.33\n0.44\n\nAI-Tech↑ AI-Sem↑\n4.60\n4.69\n4.79\n\n4.35\n4.64\n4.72\n\nanonymously shuffled system outputs per case, for 150 clips\nand 300 ordinal ratings (MOS-Q and MOS-R) per completed\nresponse. The study script randomizes clip order within each", "numLines": 500, "startLine": 1500, "totalLines": 2219}}}
