{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-990dccd8caefb6b2", "type": "tool_result", "content": "2000\tcase with seed 42, hides the system mapping, and provides\n2001\twritten 1–5 scoring instructions. The aggregation excludes\n2002\tratings with missing mappings or values outside [1, 5]. MOS\n2003\tresults are reported as mean ± standard deviation.\n2004\t\n2005\t• Scene coherence: whether components form a plausible\n2006\tintegrated scene; and\n2007\t• Technical quality: clipping, distortion, discontinuity,\n2008\tabrupt cuts, and synthesis artifacts.\n2009\tFor clean dialogue, background-adherence and acousticrelation are marked not applicable rather than treated as zeros.\n2010\tThe semantic score is the mean of all applicable first six dimensions; technical quality is retained separately.\n2011\tThe judge must return a short audible-evidence string for\n2012\tevery score, detected languages, detected speaker count, an\n2013\tordered transcript where applicable, detected background elements, and a confidence score. Outputs failing the required\n2014\tJSON schema or score ranges are rejected and retried. Temperature is zero to reduce sampling variation.\n2015\t\n2016\tD.4\n2017\t\n2018\t1.54\n2019\t1.29\n2020\t1.26\n2021\t\n2022\tCLAP↑\n2023\t\n2024\tComplex Scene\n2025\t\n2026\tE\n2027\t\n2028\tTargeted Routing Ablations\n2029\t\n2030\tE.1\n2031\t\n2032\tScope and Evaluation Protocol\n2033\t\n2034\tThe main paper compares SonicWeave with matched Dense\n2035\tand Base-MoE controls. Targeted ablations isolate two specific\n2036\tCPE-MoE design choices rather than exhaustively decomposing all architectural factors. Every ablation retains the same\n2037\tdata, backbone width/depth, number of routed layers, expert\n2038\tcount, top-K, optimizer schedule, training budget, prompt interface, and sampling protocol unless the row explicitly changes\n2039\tone variable.\n2040\tAll ablation variants are evaluated with objective metrics across TTS, TTA, TTM, and Complex-Scene referencefree judging. We report English WER and Mandarin CER,\n2041\tAudioCaps FAD/KL/CLAP, Song Describer KL/CLAP, and\n2042\tComplex-Scene AI-Tech/AI-Sem. Human MOS is reserved for\n2043\tthe main model comparison and is not used to rank targeted\n2044\tablations.\n2045\t\n2046\tHuman Listening Study\n2047\t\n2048\tThe listening study presents every system output anonymously.\n2049\tFor each case, available clips are assigned anonymous identifiers and their order is randomized with a fixed study seed.\n2050\tThe private identifier-to-system mapping is not shared with\n2051\tlisteners. Listeners score each clip independently on two 1–5\n2052\tscales:\n2053\t• MOS-Q (quality): 1 is nearly unusable due to severe noise,\n2054\tclipping, distortion, discontinuity, or unintelligibility; 2\n2055\thas major persistent artifacts that substantially impair\n2056\tlistening; 3 is basically listenable with noticeable artifacts;\n2057\t4 is clear and stable with only minor artifacts; 5 is clear,\n2058\tnatural, stable, and nearly artifact-free.\n2059\t\n2060\tE.2\n2061\t\n2062\tFixed Prior–Evidence Fusion\n2063\t\n2064\tThe fixed-fusion control replaces the learned chunk gate by\n2065\tg j = 0.5 for every valid chunk, layer, prompt, and diffusion\n2066\tphase:\n2067\tℓ j = 0.5ℓprior + 0.5ℓevid\n2068\t(24)\n2069\tj .\n2070\tIt retains C = 4 and all remaining CPE-MoE components. This\n2071\tcontrol tests whether adaptive, content- and phase-dependent\n2072\tfusion improves over a uniform average. It does not separately\n2073\tidentify the causal contribution of the prior branch or evidence\n2074\tbranch, because both are still present. The reported table shows\n2075\tthat this control is consistently worse than the learned gate:\n2076\tEnglish WER and Chinese CER increase, AudioCaps FAD/KL/CLAP degrade, Song Describer KL/CLAP degrade, and\n2077\tComplex-Scene AI-Tech/AI-Sem decrease. The degradation is\n2078\tespecially clear on TTS, which is consistent with a uniform fusion rule limiting the adaptive balance between the text-derived\n2079\tprior and evolving acoustic evidence.\n2080\t\n2081\t• MOS-R (request realization): 1 means that the requested\n2082\tscene is essentially absent or contradictory; 2 means that\n2083\tonly isolated requested elements are recognizable while\n2084\tkey content or relations are missing; 3 means major requested content is present but with clear omissions or incorrect relationships; 4 means nearly all requested content\n2085\tand relations are realized with minor omissions; 5 means\n2086\tforeground speech, music, events, ambience, timing, and\n2087\trelative prominence are jointly and naturally realized.\n2088\tThe listening study used 25 listener responses. Every listener\n2089\treceived the same questionnaire containing 25 cases and six\n2090\t17\n2091\t\n2092\t\fsinging use MusicCaps; mixed examples use prompts from\n2093\tcomplex-scene test sets. Each item is assigned a fixed prompt\n2094\tidentifier and a maximum frame length appropriate to its source,\n2095\tpreventing a content type from being overrepresented solely\n2096\tthrough longer sequences.\n2097\tFor every routed layer, generation step, and valid chunk, we\n2098\trecord the conflict gate, posterior routing distribution, and topK dispatch. All aggregate statistics are first averaged within\n2099\teach prompt and then across prompts, so that long clips or clips\n2100\tcontaining more valid chunks do not receive disproportionate\n2101\tweight.\n2102\t\n2103\tF.2 Phase- and Content-Conditioned Expert Usage\n2104\tFigure 4 reports mean expert dispatch fractions for each content\n2105\ttype during the early, middle, and late thirds of the generation\n2106\ttrajectory. The dispatch patterns depend jointly on content and\n2107\tphase. Speech retains a strong preference for E0, while singing\n2108\tconsistently uses E3 more heavily. Sound effects, music, and\n2109\tmixed scenes exhibit sharper preferences early in generation\n2110\tand become more balanced as the sample is refined. Thus, the\n2111\trouted experts do not form a single static partition of the training domains: their relative utilization evolves during generation\n2112\twhile preserving persistent content-dependent tendencies.\n2113\t\n2114\tFigure 4: Phase- and content-conditioned expert usage. Mean\n2115\tdispatch fractions are shown for each content type over the early,\n2116\tmiddle, and late thirds of the diffusion trajectory. Content-specific\n2117\tpreferences persist across phases, while the degree of concentration\n2118\tchanges as generation proceeds.\n2119\t\n2120\tE.3\n2121\t\n2122\tF.3 Does the Gate Control the Effective Router?\n2123\tThe conflict gate is designed to interpolate the prior and evidence logits, but this definition alone does not show whether\n2124\ttrained gates occupy a useful operating range or materially\n2125\talter the resulting routing distribution. We therefore bin valid\n2126\tchunks by g j and measure the Jensen–Shannon divergence\n2127\t(JSD) between the fused posterior distribution and each of its\n2128\ttwo inputs. As shown in Figure 6, low-gate chunks remain\n2129\tclose to the global prior and comparatively far from local evidence. The relation reverses for high-gate chunks: the posterior\n2130\tapproaches the evidence distribution and departs sharply from\n2131\tthe prior. Intermediate bins provide a smooth transition between these regimes. This aggregate behavior confirms that the\n2132\tlearned gate controls the effective routing distribution according to its intended prior-to-evidence semantics.\n2133\t\n2134\tChunk-Size Sensitivity\n2135\t\n2136\tThe C = 8 variant is broadly comparable to C = 4 on public benchmarks, but is consistently worse on every displayed\n2137\tTTS, AudioCaps, and Song Describer metric. The difference is\n2138\tmodest for globally coherent content, such as sustained music,\n2139\tbecause a longer chunk can still share a suitable expert path.\n2140\tHowever, each C = 8 assignment constrains twice as many\n2141\tneighboring latent frames as C = 4. This reduces the routing\n2142\tfrequency available to react to short events, speaker turns, or\n2143\tlocal changes in foreground–background balance. The effect\n2144\tis clearest on Complex-Scene, where linguistic content and\n2145\ttransient acoustic events must be jointly realized. Conversely,\n2146\tsmaller chunks would increase routing overhead and can fragment a locally coherent acoustic event. We therefore use C = 4\n2147\tas the trade-off between local adaptivity, short-range routing\n2148\tconsistency, and sparse-dispatch efficiency.\n2149\t\n2150\tF\n2151\t\n2152\tAdditional Routing Analyses\n2153\t\n2154\tF.1\n2155\t\n2156\tRouting-Analysis Protocol\n2157\t\n2158\tF.4\n2159\t\n2160\tRepresentative Mixed-Scene Routing Case\n2161\t\n2162\tFigure 5 presents a representative case from the demo\n2163\tset (prompt identifier cpe_010: “In a busy gym, a\n2164\ttrainer shouts, ‘Three more reps, you’ve got\n2165\tthis! Keep your core tight. Two more!’ Weight\n2166\tplates clank, treadmills run, people breathe\n2167\theavily, and battle ropes strike the floor\n2168\taround him.”) The scene combines foreground speech\n2169\twith continuous machine and breathing noise, together with\n2170\ttransient or quasi-periodic impacts. It therefore provides\n2171\ta natural test of within-clip routing under overlapping\n2172\theterogeneous content.\n2173\t\n2174\tWe construct a balanced 250-prompt routing benchmark with\n2175\t50 prompts each for speech, sound effects, music, singing, and\n2176\tmixed scenes. Speech is balanced across SeedTTS English and\n2177\tChinese examples; sound effects use AudioCaps; music and\n2178\t18\n2179\t\n2180\t\fFigure 5: Conflict-gate dynamics and mixed-scene routing. The gym scene cpe_010, showing the gate averaged over four CPE layers and\n2181\tLayer 13 top-1 expert assignments. Complex background regions rely more on acoustic evidence, while the main speech segment shows stronger\n2182\treliance on the text prior.\n2183\t\n2184\tevidence. The Layer 13 dispatch trace also switches experts\n2185\tmore frequently around local acoustic changes, consistent with\n2186\tchunk-level adaptation to heterogeneous and transient content.\n2187\tAround 7 seconds, the main speech segment becomes prominent in both the spectrogram and the corresponding demo audio.\n2188\tThis transition coincides with a sustained decrease of the mean\n2189\tgate below 0.5, shifting the routing toward the structured text\n2190\tprior, which provides a stable description of the foreground\n2191\tspeech. The expert assignments simultaneously enter longer,\n2192\tmore stable runs. Near the end of the clip, as the background\n2193\tactivity becomes more prominent again, the gate rises and the\n2194\texpert pattern changes accordingly.\n2195\tAlthough the correspondence is not frame-exact, the case\n2196\tillustrates the intended behavior of CPE-MoE: local acoustic\n2197\tevidence plays a larger role in complex and rapidly changing\n2198\tregions, whereas the global text prior becomes more influential\n2199\tduring semantically well-specified speech segments.\n2200\t\n2201\tFigure 6: Gate-controlled prior–evidence routing. Valid chunks are\n2202\tgrouped by their conflict-gate values. At low g j , the fused posterior is\n2203\tclose to the global prior; at high g j , it is close to local evidence. JSD\n2204\tdenotes Jensen–Shannon divergence.\n2205\t\n2206\tThe upper trace shows the conflict gate averaged over the\n2207\tfour CPE-MoE layers, whereas the colored strip visualizes top1 dispatch from a single routed layer (Layer 13). We include\n2208\tone layer to keep the visualization readable; the layer is used\n2209\tonly as an illustrative dispatch trace and is not intended to\n2210\tsummarize routing behavior across all routed layers.\n2211\tThe trace shows a coarse but interpretable correspondence\n2212\tbetween the acoustic composition and the routing behavior.\n2213\tDuring the earlier portion of the clip, the scene is dominated by\n2214\tdense background activity, including machine noise, breathing,\n2215\tand transient impacts. In this region, the mean gate is mostly\n2216\tabove 0.5, indicating stronger reliance on the evolving acoustic\n2217\t19\n2218\t\n2219\t\f\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "99f9f2f5-463f-4d73-8c1b-6ee352cfb6b2", "uuid": "87174555-6664-417f-bd7f-3d2a2ce9738d", "timestamp": "2026-08-11T14:44:50.615Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/sonicweave.txt", "content": "case with seed 42, hides the system mapping, and provides\nwritten 1–5 scoring instructions. The aggregation excludes\nratings with missing mappings or values outside [1, 5]. MOS\nresults are reported as mean ± standard deviation.\n\n• Scene coherence: whether components form a plausible\nintegrated scene; and\n• Technical quality: clipping, distortion, discontinuity,\nabrupt cuts, and synthesis artifacts.\nFor clean dialogue, background-adherence and acousticrelation are marked not applicable rather than treated as zeros.\nThe semantic score is the mean of all applicable first six dimensions; technical quality is retained separately.\nThe judge must return a short audible-evidence string for\nevery score, detected languages, detected speaker count, an\nordered transcript where applicable, detected background elements, and a confidence score. Outputs failing the required\nJSON schema or score ranges are rejected and retried. Temperature is zero to reduce sampling variation.\n\nD.4\n\n1.54\n1.29\n1.26\n\nCLAP↑\n\nComplex Scene\n\nE\n\nTargeted Routing Ablations\n\nE.1\n\nScope and Evaluation Protocol\n\nThe main paper compares SonicWeave with matched Dense\nand Base-MoE controls. Targeted ablations isolate two specific\nCPE-MoE design choices rather than exhaustively decomposing all architectural factors. Every ablation retains the same\ndata, backbone width/depth, number of routed layers, expert\ncount, top-K, optimizer schedule, training budget, prompt interface, and sampling protocol unless the row explicitly changes\none variable.\nAll ablation variants are evaluated with objective metrics across TTS, TTA, TTM, and Complex-Scene referencefree judging. We report English WER and Mandarin CER,\nAudioCaps FAD/KL/CLAP, Song Describer KL/CLAP, and\nComplex-Scene AI-Tech/AI-Sem. Human MOS is reserved for\nthe main model comparison and is not used to rank targeted\nablations.\n\nHuman Listening Study\n\nThe listening study presents every system output anonymously.\nFor each case, available clips are assigned anonymous identifiers and their order is randomized with a fixed study seed.\nThe private identifier-to-system mapping is not shared with\nlisteners. Listeners score each clip independently on two 1–5\nscales:\n• MOS-Q (quality): 1 is nearly unusable due to severe noise,\nclipping, distortion, discontinuity, or unintelligibility; 2\nhas major persistent artifacts that substantially impair\nlistening; 3 is basically listenable with noticeable artifacts;\n4 is clear and stable with only minor artifacts; 5 is clear,\nnatural, stable, and nearly artifact-free.\n\nE.2\n\nFixed Prior–Evidence Fusion\n\nThe fixed-fusion control replaces the learned chunk gate by\ng j = 0.5 for every valid chunk, layer, prompt, and diffusion\nphase:\nℓ j = 0.5ℓprior + 0.5ℓevid\n(24)\nj .\nIt retains C = 4 and all remaining CPE-MoE components. This\ncontrol tests whether adaptive, content- and phase-dependent\nfusion improves over a uniform average. It does not separately\nidentify the causal contribution of the prior branch or evidence\nbranch, because both are still present. The reported table shows\nthat this control is consistently worse than the learned gate:\nEnglish WER and Chinese CER increase, AudioCaps FAD/KL/CLAP degrade, Song Describer KL/CLAP degrade, and\nComplex-Scene AI-Tech/AI-Sem decrease. The degradation is\nespecially clear on TTS, which is consistent with a uniform fusion rule limiting the adaptive balance between the text-derived\nprior and evolving acoustic evidence.\n\n• MOS-R (request realization): 1 means that the requested\nscene is essentially absent or contradictory; 2 means that\nonly isolated requested elements are recognizable while\nkey content or relations are missing; 3 means major requested content is present but with clear omissions or incorrect relationships; 4 means nearly all requested content\nand relations are realized with minor omissions; 5 means\nforeground speech, music, events, ambience, timing, and\nrelative prominence are jointly and naturally realized.\nThe listening study used 25 listener responses. Every listener\nreceived the same questionnaire containing 25 cases and six\n17\n\n\fsinging use MusicCaps; mixed examples use prompts from\ncomplex-scene test sets. Each item is assigned a fixed prompt\nidentifier and a maximum frame length appropriate to its source,\npreventing a content type from being overrepresented solely\nthrough longer sequences.\nFor every routed layer, generation step, and valid chunk, we\nrecord the conflict gate, posterior routing distribution, and topK dispatch. All aggregate statistics are first averaged within\neach prompt and then across prompts, so that long clips or clips\ncontaining more valid chunks do not receive disproportionate\nweight.\n\nF.2 Phase- and Content-Conditioned Expert Usage\nFigure 4 reports mean expert dispatch fractions for each content\ntype during the early, middle, and late thirds of the generation\ntrajectory. The dispatch patterns depend jointly on content and\nphase. Speech retains a strong preference for E0, while singing\nconsistently uses E3 more heavily. Sound effects, music, and\nmixed scenes exhibit sharper preferences early in generation\nand become more balanced as the sample is refined. Thus, the\nrouted experts do not form a single static partition of the training domains: their relative utilization evolves during generation\nwhile preserving persistent content-dependent tendencies.\n\nFigure 4: Phase- and content-conditioned expert usage. Mean\ndispatch fractions are shown for each content type over the early,\nmiddle, and late thirds of the diffusion trajectory. Content-specific\npreferences persist across phases, while the degree of concentration\nchanges as generation proceeds.\n\nE.3\n\nF.3 Does the Gate Control the Effective Router?\nThe conflict gate is designed to interpolate the prior and evidence logits, but this definition alone does not show whether\ntrained gates occupy a useful operating range or materially\nalter the resulting routing distribution. We therefore bin valid\nchunks by g j and measure the Jensen–Shannon divergence\n(JSD) between the fused posterior distribution and each of its\ntwo inputs. As shown in Figure 6, low-gate chunks remain\nclose to the global prior and comparatively far from local evidence. The relation reverses for high-gate chunks: the posterior\napproaches the evidence distribution and departs sharply from\nthe prior. Intermediate bins provide a smooth transition between these regimes. This aggregate behavior confirms that the\nlearned gate controls the effective routing distribution according to its intended prior-to-evidence semantics.\n\nChunk-Size Sensitivity\n\nThe C = 8 variant is broadly comparable to C = 4 on public benchmarks, but is consistently worse on every displayed\nTTS, AudioCaps, and Song Describer metric. The difference is\nmodest for globally coherent content, such as sustained music,\nbecause a longer chunk can still share a suitable expert path.\nHowever, each C = 8 assignment constrains twice as many\nneighboring latent frames as C = 4. This reduces the routing\nfrequency available to react to short events, speaker turns, or\nlocal changes in foreground–background balance. The effect\nis clearest on Complex-Scene, where linguistic content and\ntransient acoustic events must be jointly realized. Conversely,\nsmaller chunks would increase routing overhead and can fragment a locally coherent acoustic event. We therefore use C = 4\nas the trade-off between local adaptivity, short-range routing\nconsistency, and sparse-dispatch efficiency.\n\nF\n\nAdditional Routing Analyses\n\nF.1\n\nRouting-Analysis Protocol\n\nF.4\n\nRepresentative Mixed-Scene Routing Case\n\nFigure 5 presents a representative case from the demo\nset (prompt identifier cpe_010: “In a busy gym, a\ntrainer shouts, ‘Three more reps, you’ve got\nthis! Keep your core tight. Two more!’ Weight\nplates clank, treadmills run, people breathe\nheavily, and battle ropes strike the floor\naround him.”) The scene combines foreground speech\nwith continuous machine and breathing noise, together with\ntransient or quasi-periodic impacts. It therefore provides\na natural test of within-clip routing under overlapping\nheterogeneous content.\n\nWe construct a balanced 250-prompt routing benchmark with\n50 prompts each for speech, sound effects, music, singing, and\nmixed scenes. Speech is balanced across SeedTTS English and\nChinese examples; sound effects use AudioCaps; music and\n18\n\n\fFigure 5: Conflict-gate dynamics and mixed-scene routing. The gym scene cpe_010, showing the gate averaged over four CPE layers and\nLayer 13 top-1 expert assignments. Complex background regions rely more on acoustic evidence, while the main speech segment shows stronger\nreliance on the text prior.\n\nevidence. The Layer 13 dispatch trace also switches experts\nmore frequently around local acoustic changes, consistent with\nchunk-level adaptation to heterogeneous and transient content.\nAround 7 seconds, the main speech segment becomes prominent in both the spectrogram and the corresponding demo audio.\nThis transition coincides with a sustained decrease of the mean\ngate below 0.5, shifting the routing toward the structured text\nprior, which provides a stable description of the foreground\nspeech. The expert assignments simultaneously enter longer,\nmore stable runs. Near the end of the clip, as the background\nactivity becomes more prominent again, the gate rises and the\nexpert pattern changes accordingly.\nAlthough the correspondence is not frame-exact, the case\nillustrates the intended behavior of CPE-MoE: local acoustic\nevidence plays a larger role in complex and rapidly changing\nregions, whereas the global text prior becomes more influential\nduring semantically well-specified speech segments.\n\nFigure 6: Gate-controlled prior–evidence routing. Valid chunks are\ngrouped by their conflict-gate values. At low g j , the fused posterior is\nclose to the global prior; at high g j , it is close to local evidence. JSD\ndenotes Jensen–Shannon divergence.\n\nThe upper trace shows the conflict gate averaged over the\nfour CPE-MoE layers, whereas the colored strip visualizes top1 dispatch from a single routed layer (Layer 13). We include\none layer to keep the visualization readable; the layer is used\nonly as an illustrative dispatch trace and is not intended to\nsummarize routing behavior across all routed layers.\nThe trace shows a coarse but interpretable correspondence\nbetween the acoustic composition and the routing behavior.\nDuring the earlier portion of the clip, the scene is dominated by\ndense background activity, including machine noise, breathing,\nand transient impacts. In this region, the mean gate is mostly\nabove 0.5, indicating stronger reliance on the evolving acoustic\n19\n\n\f", "numLines": 220, "startLine": 2000, "totalLines": 2219}}}
